{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/matters-A2.json docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/libraries-A2.json docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/opening-articles-A2.json'","aggregated_output":"[\n {\n  \"name\": \"NVIDIA fiscal 2027 revenue (total, or its data-center segment, about 92% of it)\",\n  \"example_question\": \"What will NVIDIA report as data-center revenue for fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027), the segment carrying ~92% of total revenue, against expected ~90% total revenue growth?\",\n  \"articles\": [\n   3,\n   6,\n   13,\n   18,\n   21\n  ],\n  \"listed_by\": \"both (Codex: total revenue; Claude: data-center revenue)\",\n  \"matter\": \"M01\"\n },\n {\n  \"name\": \"NVIDIA fiscal 2028 revenue (total, or its data-center segment)\",\n  \"example_question\": \"What will NVIDIA report as data-center revenue for fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028), against the company's projected ~70% revenue growth?\",\n  \"articles\": [\n   3,\n   13,\n   18,\n   21,\n   29,\n   39\n  ],\n  \"listed_by\": \"both (Codex: total revenue; Claude: data-center revenue)\",\n  \"matter\": \"M02\"\n },\n {\n  \"name\": \"NVIDIA fiscal 2028 non-GAAP gross margin against the 72%-73% outlook\",\n  \"example_question\": \"What will NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin be for fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028), against the CFO's 72%-73% outlook as price hikes take effect?\",\n  \"articles\": [\n   21\n  ],\n  \"listed_by\": \"both\",\n  \"matter\": \"M03\"\n },\n {\n  \"name\": \"NVIDIA supply and capacity purchase commitments at fiscal 2027 year-end ($279B at Q2)\",\n  \"example_question\": \"How large will NVIDIA's supply and capacity purchase commitments be at the end of fiscal 2027 (late January 2027), after rising from $119B to $279B in Q2?\",\n  \"articles\": [\n   18,\n   29\n  ],\n  \"listed_by\": \"both\",\n  \"matter\": \"M04\"\n },\n {\n  \"name\": \"Whether Beijing formally approves Chinese firms buying NVIDIA RTX Pro 5500 GPUs by 2027-01-31\",\n  \"example_question\": \"Will China's government formally approve domestic firms such as Alibaba and ByteDance buying NVIDIA's RTX Pro 5500 GPUs by 2027-01-31 (researcher-set check date: NVIDIA's fiscal year-end; no date given)?\",\n  \"articles\": [\n   31\n  ],\n  \"listed_by\": \"both\",\n  \"matter\": \"M05\"\n },\n {\n  \"name\": \"NVIDIA fiscal 2027 non-GAAP gross margin under memory-cost squeeze (Q4 trough 71%-72%)\",\n  \"example_question\": \"What will NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin be for fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027), given estimates of a fall from 75% to under 72% by Q4 on memory costs?\",\n  \"articles\": [\n   3,\n   21\n  ],\n  \"listed_by\": \"one reader\",\n  \"matter\": \"M06\"\n },\n {\n  \"name\": \"Whether Vera Rubin begins revenue-bearing production shipments in Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n  \"example_question\": \"Will NVIDIA confirm that Vera Rubin systems began revenue-bearing production shipments in Q3 fiscal 2027 (quarter ending late October 2026)?\",\n  \"articles\": [\n   6,\n   13,\n   18\n  ],\n  \"listed_by\": \"one reader\",\n  \"matter\": \"M07\"\n },\n {\n  \"name\": \"Whether AMD's 2027 data-center revenue more than doubles as Helios/MI450 ramps\",\n  \"example_question\": \"Will AMD's data-center segment revenue in calendar 2027 more than double its 2026 level, as AMD expects with the Helios/MI450 ramp?\",\n  \"articles\": [\n   18,\n   20\n  ],\n  \"listed_by\": \"one reader\",\n  \"matter\": \"M08\"\n }\n]{\n \"X\": [\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"X-1\",\n   \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n   \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-1 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"X-2\",\n   \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\",\n   \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-2 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"X-3\",\n   \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\",\n   \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-3 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"X-4\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\",\n   \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article 6 of the packet\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"X-5\",\n   \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\",\n   \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-4 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"X-6\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\",\n   \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-5 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"X-7\",\n   \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\",\n   \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-6 (outside the 40)\"\n  }\n ],\n \"Y\": [\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"Y-1\",\n   \"question\": \"At NVIDIA’s next annual report, what amount of financing commitments to customers and partners will NVIDIA report in its commitments note?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The amount of NVIDIA’s credit support, guarantees and other financing commitments to customers and partners\",\n   \"period\": \"due 2027-02-28, year FY2027\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-1 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"Y-2\",\n   \"question\": \"How much total revenue will Nvidia report for FY2027?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Nvidia total revenue in FY2027\",\n   \"period\": \"due 2027-02-28, year FY2027\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-7 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"Y-3\",\n   \"question\": \"How much total revenue will Nvidia report for FY2028?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Nvidia total revenue in FY2028\",\n   \"period\": \"due 2028-02-29, year FY2028\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-7 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"Y-4\",\n   \"question\": \"How much data-center segment revenue will Nvidia report for FY2027?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Nvidia data-center segment revenue\",\n   \"period\": \"due 2027-02-28, year FY2027\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-8 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"Y-5\",\n   \"question\": \"How much will Nvidia's total revenue grow in FY2028?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"FY2028 total revenue growth\",\n   \"period\": \"due 2028-02-29, year FY2028\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-9 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"Y-6\",\n   \"question\": \"Will the U.S. administration authorize Nvidia Blackwell chip exports to China by January 31, 2027?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether the U.S. authorizes Nvidia Blackwell chip exports to China\",\n   \"period\": \"due 2027-01-31, year 2027\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-3 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"Y-7\",\n   \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Combined data-center capital spending by the five largest AI hyperscalers in calendar 2026\",\n   \"period\": \"due 2027-04-30, year 2026\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-4 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"Y-8\",\n   \"question\": \"How much will tech companies spend on AI in calendar 2027?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Annual AI spending by tech companies\",\n   \"period\": \"due 2028-12-31, year 2027\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-10 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"Y-9\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform begin shipping by January 31, 2027?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Will NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform begin shipping by January 31, 2027?\",\n   \"period\": \"due 2027-01-31, year 2027\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-11 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"Y-10\",\n   \"question\": \"What gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2028?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"NVIDIA FY2028 gross margin\",\n   \"period\": \"due 2028-02-28, year FY2028\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-12 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"Y-11\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA begin shipping Rubin GPUs by December 31, 2027?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA Rubin GPUs begin shipping by the end of calendar 2027, as indicated by AWS’s planned deployments.\",\n   \"period\": \"due 2027-12-31, year 2027\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-13 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"Y-12\",\n   \"question\": \"At NVIDIA’s FY2027 annual report, what amount of purchase commitments for the Hugging Face acquisition will NVIDIA report in its commitments note?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"NVIDIA’s purchase commitment for the Hugging Face acquisition\",\n   \"period\": \"due 2027-02-28, year FY2027\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-5 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"Y-13\",\n   \"question\": \"Will Nvidia agree to invest in Anthropic's IPO as an anchor investor by September 26, 2027?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether Nvidia takes an equity stake as an anchor investor in Anthropic's IPO\",\n   \"period\": \"due 2027-09-26, year 2027\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-14 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"Y-14\",\n   \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology permit Alibaba and ByteDance to buy Nvidia RTX PRO 5500 cards by January 31, 2027?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Chinese permission for Alibaba and ByteDance to purchase Nvidia RTX PRO 5500 cards\",\n   \"period\": \"due 2027-01-31, year 2027\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article O-15 (outside the 40)\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"system_id\": \"Y-15\",\n   \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology approve domestic firms' purchases of Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 GPUs by January 31, 2027?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Chinese approval of domestic firms' purchases of Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 GPUs\",\n   \"period\": \"due 2027-01-31, year 2027\",\n   \"opened_from\": \"article 31 of the packet\"\n  }\n ]\n}[\n {\n  \"article\": \"article O-1 (outside the 40)\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n  \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Is NVIDIA Funding Its Own Stock Rally?\",\n  \"text\": \"Is NVIDIA Funding Its Own Stock Rally?\\nIf you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet. NVIDIA’s revenue more than doubled year over year in fiscal Q2 2027, so this number is easy to miss. An important thing to watch is how much future demand depends on NVIDIA backing its own customers.\\nWhy Does NVIDIA Put Its Own Money Behind Its Customers?\\nHere is how this kind of support works, as a made-up example. A chipmaker invests in a young customer. That customer then spends the money on the chipmaker’s products.\\nThe sale is real, but the chipmaker’s own money sits on both sides of it. Management said in the fiscal Q2 2027 call that some will call NVIDIA’s support circular financing.\\nManagement says a surge in AI demand is driving a global buildout of computing infrastructure. It also describes revenue-sharing deals with cloud partners.\\nManagement says these deals matter because, over time, they can expand NVIDIA’s addressable market. They can also create a recurring revenue stream tied to usage. That explains why NVIDIA does it, but not how big the support has become.\\nHow Big Has NVIDIA’s Support For Its Customers Become?\\nNVIDIA has already invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, according to management. For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity. With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\\nThose lab investments are part of a broader expansion: Business Insider reported that NVIDIA’s overall equity portfolio has reached $99 billion. In summer 2025, that portfolio was roughly $7 billion. That is a 14-fold increase, so this is not a mild change.\\nThere is also a change in how quickly NVIDIA gets paid. Days of sales outstanding, the average time to collect cash after a sale, increased to 60 days. Management said this reflects extended payment terms for large purchases by certain investment-grade customers, shipped over multiple quarters. So some buyers with strong credit are taking longer to pay.\\nWhat Are You Paying For NVIDIA Stock Today?\\nNVIDIA stock trades at 28.1 times its past year’s earnings. The S&P 500 trades at 22.5 times. The multiple also sits in the bottom tenth of NVIDIA’s own 10-year range.\\nThe price likely assumes that growth holds up and that the backed customers keep paying . Management points to the size of its customers’ commitments. OpenAI’s existing and planned commitments represent about 12 gigawatts of NVIDIA computing. OpenAI has committed to deployments through 2030. Whether those purchases rest on NVIDIA’s backing is worth watching.\\nNVIDIA’s own finances are also strong. Its debt is just 0.7% of its market value. For the S&P 500 as a whole, that figure is 21%, so NVIDIA carries far less debt.\\nRoughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back. That is a notable share, and it represents a key risk factor that investors should monitor. The worry would grow if that share climbs or collection times keep stretching. It would ease if more growth comes from customers paying their own way. NVIDIA’s next update comes at its fiscal Q3 2027 earnings call on November 17.\\nLearn more about NVDA | Identify catalysts | Generate income against NVDA stock\\nTrim your NVDA position | Tax-efficient rebalancing strategies | Opt for High Quality Portfolio\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": \"article O-2 (outside the 40)\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n  \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Top Analyst Reports for NVIDIA, Berkshire Hathaway & GE Aerospace\",\n  \"text\": \"Tuesday, September 22, 2026\\nThe Zacks Research Daily presents the best research output of our analyst team. Today's Research Daily features new research reports on 16 major stocks, including NVIDIA Corp. (NVDA), Berkshire Hathaway Inc. (BRK.B) and GE Aerospace (GE). These research reports have been hand-picked from the roughly 70 reports published by our analyst team today.\\nYou can see all of today's research reports here >>>\\nThe daily 'Ahead of Wall Street' article is a must-read for all investors who would like to be ready for that day's trading action. The article comes out before the market opens, attempting to make sense of that morning's economic releases and how they will affect that day's market action. You can read this article for free on our home page and can actually sign up there to get an email notification as this article comes out each morning.\\nYou can read today's AWS here >>> Still Waters Bring Mixed Pre-Market Levels\\nShares of NVIDIA have gained +27.8% over the past year against the Zacks Semiconductor - General industry's gain of +35.6%. The company continues to benefit from broad demand for accelerated computing as customers build AI factories across hyperscalers, AI clouds, enterprises and sovereign buyers.\\nBlackwell Ultra is supporting growth while Vera Rubin broadens the platform across GPUs, CPUs, networking and software. Customer diversification, rising system content and usage-linked opportunities extend the runway, while cash generation supports capital returns. China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\\nCustomer concentration, rising operating investment and competition add uncertainty. Even so, platform leadership, product cadence and expanding AI infrastructure demand give the positive drivers greater weight in the current risk-reward profile.\\n(You can read the full research report on NVIDIA here >>> )\\nBerkshire Hathaway's shares have gained +1.6% over the past year against the Zacks Insurance - Property and Casualty industry's gain of +2.8%. The company benefits from diversified businesses, disciplined underwriting and a large liquidity base that support durable earnings capacity and flexible capital allocation. Insurance float remains a low-cost funding source, while manufacturing, services, BNSF and Berkshire Hathaway Energy broaden the earnings mix.\\nRecent acquisitions and renewed share repurchases show multiple avenues for deploying capital. Still, insurance results remain exposed to catastrophe losses and rising claim frequency and severity. Lower short-term rates are reducing insurance investment income, while BNSF and BHE require substantial capital spending.\\nGreg Abel is now leading the company, leaving execution under the new leadership structure as an important watchpoint. These offsets support an overall balanced risk-reward profile and a Neutral view.\\n(You can read the full research report on Berkshire Hathaway here >>> )\\nShares of GE Aerospace have outperformed the Zacks Aerospace - Defense industry over the past year (+4.9% vs. -13.2%). The company has been witnessing strength in its businesses, driven by robust demand for commercial engines, propulsion and additive technologies. Rising U.S. & international defense budgets, geopolitical tensions, positive airline & airframer dynamics and robust demand for commercial air travel augur well for the company.\\nGE Aerospace's portfolio-reshaping actions are likely to unlock value for its shareholders. The company raised its dividend by 30.6% to 36 cents per share in February 2026.\\nHowever, it has been dealing with high costs and expenses related to certain projects and restructuring activities, which are likely to affect its margins and profitability. Supply-chain disruptions may also result in delays and increased costs. Given GE Aerospace's international presence, foreign exchange headwinds might be worrying for the company.\\n(You can read the full research report on GE Aerospace here >>> )\\nOther noteworthy reports we are featuring today include Intel Corp. (INTC), Morgan Stanley (MS) and Arm Holdings plc (ARM).\\nPer the Zacks analyst, strong demand for AI and data-center infrastructure is likely to support Intel's top line. Its focus on AI-ready computing and advanced networking solutions is positive.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, rising adoption of compute platforms across cloud artificial intelligence is benefiting ARM. However, Elevated expenses due to increased investments remain a concern.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, Dollar Tree is benefiting from broad category growth, multi-price adoption and store improvements, while measured expansion and reinvestment support future sales.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, Textron gains from diversified exposure across business aviation, helicopters, defense platforms and aftermarket services, supported by strong demand and defense modernization.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, Antero Resources' expansion of its core West Virginia Marcellus position enhances its production outlook. However, its exposure to commodity price volatility raises concerns.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, Hexcel Corporation is likely to benefit from the improving air traffic. Yet labor shortage result in delays and likely impact operating results.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, SL Green benefits from strong leasing, rising rents and broad tenant demand. However, elevated debt, interest expenses and refinancing exposure remain material risks.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, solid IB business, strategic alliances/acquisitions, global footprint and Morgan Stanley's focus on less capital-markets-dependent operations will likely support its financials.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, growing 800G demand and 1.6T FiberEdge and CopperEdge ramps are driving Semtech's data center growth.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, strong Xifaxan growth, strategic acquisitions and new product approvals fuel Bausch Health's growth prospects. However, uncertainty around Bausch + Lomb separation is a concern.\\nThe Zacks analyst is concerned with Boston Scientific facing a sharp U.S. WATCHMAN market slowdown, as higher clinical evidence impacts referral trends. Portfolio gaps within CRM are also worrying.\\nPer the Zacks Analyst, Rigetti faces rising losses, execution and dilution risks, though cloud expansion, Novera sales, government ties and a solid cash position support longer-term potential.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, NVR faces housing affordability constraints, softer settlements and margin pressure. Also, lower home prices and higher lot costs remain concerns.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": \"article O-3 (outside the 40)\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n  \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Will Nvidia be allowed to sell more chips to China? This former Trump economic adviser has a hot take.\",\n  \"text\": \"Will Nvidia be allowed to sell more chips to China? This former Trump economic adviser has a hot take.\\nWhen Nvidia ( NVDA ) CEO Jensen Huang arrives at the state dinner on Thursday between President Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping, you better believe he'll be hoping to get a sense of when — and to what extent — his business may resume in China.\\nBoth sides coming to a deal on this front won't be easy, given the politics still at play.\\n\\\"My personal opinion is that we need to have as much competitive advantage here in the United States as we can, because we've got competitive disadvantages against China,\\\" said Gary Cohn, IBM ( IBM ) vice chair and former economic adviser to President Trump in his first term, on Yahoo Finance's Sozzi Unleashed . \\\"You know, they don't have environmental issues. They don't have a cost of capital. They can build data centers wherever they want. The citizens can't stand up and say not in my backyard.\\\"\\nCohn added, \\\"To the extent that the Chinese have all these other significant advantages, the one advantage we have is we've got the chips, and we've got the chip manufacturing here. I'd like to keep that advantage as long as we can. \\\"\\nThe Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China. The problem for Nvidia: The H200 is already an older-generation product, trailing Nvidia's current Blackwell chips in terms of performance.\\nIn other words, Nvidia is likely leaving billions in sales on the table by being unable to get Blackwell chips into China to feed the country's AI startup scene.\\nChinese authorities, however, have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips.\\nWhile Nvidia hasn't factored any sales from China into its outlook for the year, the stock is still up 21% year to date, powered by the AI boom here in the US.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": \"article 6 of the packet\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n  \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Sandisk vs. NVIDIA: Which AI Stock Is the Better Buy Now?\",\n  \"text\": \"Lately, surging artificial intelligence (AI) and data center demand has benefited both NVIDIA Corporation NVDA and the much smaller Sandisk Corporation SNDK. While the two companies offer different ways to capitalize on the AI boom, which one stands out to be the better buy now? Let's explore –\\nSandisk's AI-Driven Growth Boosts Revenues and Profitability\\nTotal revenues for Sandisk reached $8.97 billion in the fiscal fourth quarter of 2026, up a whopping 372% year over year and 51% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 5 press release.\\nFor fiscal year 2026, revenues reached $20.25 billion, representing 175% year-over-year growth. The Data Center segment has been the major growth driver, with revenues jumping 437% as AI and data center demand increased the need for high-value storage.\\nIn the fourth fiscal quarter, revenue growth came more from higher pricing than higher volumes. As a result, gross margins rose to 84.6% compared to 26.2% a year ago.\\nFor fiscal year 2026, gross margin was 71.5% compared to 30.1% a year earlier. Profitability also improved dramatically, with GAAP net income coming in at $11.43 billion versus a loss of $1.64 billion in the previous year.\\nThe growth doesn't seem to be temporary, as management remains bullish on their near-term outlook. They expect revenues to be between $10.3 billion and $10.8 billion in the fiscal first quarter of 2027, which would mean another strong quarterly growth following a huge fiscal fourth quarter. Now that the company has signed five more New Business Model agreements, revenue growth and profitability will surely improve.\\nNVIDIA's AI Demand Drives Strong Growth and Margin Expansion\\nThe Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\\nNVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago. Strong operating performance helped NVIDIA convert robust revenue growth into solid earnings growth.\\nNVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027. At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise. Additionally, the next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning NVIDIA to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment.\\nBoth Sandisk and NVIDIA have delivered exceptional earnings growth, fueled by an increase in AI and data center demand, resulting in sharp revenue gains and substantial margin expansion. Both companies also look quite confident about their near-term outlooks.\\nHowever, Sandisk is in a highly cyclical memory business. If supply catches up with demand, memory prices may come under pressure and weigh on margins. Moreover, the company's fast-paced growth has raised expectations, which has made its growth trajectory susceptible if pricing momentum weakens.\\nSince Sandisk's business is dependent on storage demand, it has created greater concentration risk. In contrast, NVIDIA is capitalizing on the broader AI ecosystem, has dominant data center exposure, and continues to deliver exceptional AI-driven growth. To top it off, NVIDIA's 63.7% net profit margin compared with Sandisk's 56.5%, indicates its greater efficiency in converting revenues into bottom-line profits.\\nTaken together, these factors make NVIDIA a better buy than Sandisk at the moment. NVIDIA currently has a Zacks Rank #1 (Strong Buy), while Sandisk has a Zacks Rank #3 (Hold). You can see the complete list of today's Zacks Rank #1 stocks here.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": \"article O-4 (outside the 40)\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n  \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n  \"title\": \"I've Held Nvidia for Nearly 3 Years. Here's Whether I'd Still Buy Today.\",\n  \"text\": \"Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has been one of the best stocks to own in the artificial intelligence (AI) arms race. It is up nearly 1,500% since 2023 began, providing investors with incredible returns. Unfortunately, I do not have those returns in my portfolio.\\nI bought the stock at the beginning of 2023 and watched it double. Then I sold the stock because I was worried that AI computing demand wouldn't last. I was very wrong. I had to disregard all of the prior gains and purchase shares in 2024 after it had run up some more, which has still been a great investment for me.\\nBut would I buy the stock again today? You bet I would.\\nNvidia is still a dominant force in the industry\\nNvidia is turning the investment world on its head. Normally, the law of large numbers dictates that the larger a company is, the harder it is to grow. Nvidia is the world's largest company and has reached heights that no other company has ever achieved. However, it's growing faster than any other stock among the world's top 10 companies. That's hard to believe, but it showcases just how massive the demand is for AI computing power.\\nNvidia makes GPUs , which are the go-to computing unit for all sorts of AI operations, be it training or inference. While there are alternatives, the Nvidia ecosystem cannot be beaten. Additionally, custom AI chips are starting to become more popular among many AI hyperscalers. Still, there will always be a place for computing units like Nvidia's GPUs that are flexible and can handle a wide variety of workloads.\\nDue to Nvidia's market position, it has advanced knowledge of how much computing power the AI hyperscalers are planning. The AI companies are making multiyear plans to ensure they have mapped out when they will need computing units, because data centers don't go up overnight. This gives Nvidia the ability to issue long-term guidance, and the guidance regarding the AI build-out is simply incredible.\\nNvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030. Considering the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year, that's huge growth still to come.\\nSo, if you think you've missed the boat on Nvidia, think again. There is still plenty of upside to come, and Nvidia will be one of the biggest beneficiaries of the AI build-out. However, none of it is priced into Nvidia's stock.\\nThat's pretty much in line with where other big tech stocks trade when one-time effects don't skew their valuations, but there's one problem: Nvidia is growing far faster than any of its big tech peers. The trailing price-to-earnings ratio doesn't account for Nvidia's massive growth ahead, and that's where a forward-looking metric helps out.\\nAt less than 15 times next year's earnings, Nvidia's stock has almost no growth priced into it today. That means the stock has major upside over the next year. Furthermore, with the AI build-out expected to intensify through 2030, that leaves plenty more room for Nvidia to run.\\nEven if you've missed out on Nvidia so far, now is the perfect time to buy more. Very little growth has been priced in, and Nvidia's stock looks well positioned to be one of the best investments over the next few years.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": \"article O-5 (outside the 40)\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n  \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Credo vs. Nvidia: Which AI Chip Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026?\",\n  \"text\": \"The artificial intelligence boom has transformed the semiconductor landscape. Deciding between a high-growth connectivity specialist like Credo Technology Group ( CRDO +5.75% ) and the industry giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) requires a look at their specific niches.\\nBoth companies are central to modern data centers, but they solve different problems. Credo provides the high-speed wiring and connectivity chips that keep data moving, while Nvidia supplies the massive processing power AI models require. Comparing these two reveals how different players profit from the same infrastructure trend.\\nCredo designs high-speed copper and optical interconnect solutions essential for modern AI-driven infrastructure. The company operates as a niche provider of high-speed connectivity solutions among semiconductor stocks serving AI data centers. Credo relies on a limited number of customers for a substantial portion of its revenue, with sales to its top 10 customers accounting for approximately 90% of total revenue in fiscal 2026. This level of customer concentration adds risk to the business. The company also maintains a partnership with Oracle ( ORCL -0.13% ) to develop ZeroFlap optics to improve reliability in AI data centers.\\nFinancial performance has accelerated significantly recently. In fiscal 2026, which ended in May 2026, revenue reached nearly $1.3 billion, up roughly 205.7% from the previous year. This growth helped the company achieve a net income of approximately $472.3 million. The net margin for the period was approximately 35.4%, a substantial improvement from the 11.9% net margin reported in the prior fiscal year.\\nThe balance sheet remains highly liquid. As of its May 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio (a measure of short-term debt-paying ability) is nearly 10.2x. The debt-to-equity ratio (total debt compared to equity) is 0.0x, indicating no debt relative to equity. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) was nearly $407.0 million. Stock-based compensation represented roughly 39.3% of operating cash flow, which inflates reported cash generation because SBC is a non-cash expense added back in the cash flow statement.\\nNvidia is the dominant provider of GPU-accelerated computing platforms used in gaming, professional visualization, and data centers. The company sells its products to massive cloud service providers and AI model makers globally. Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds a layer of risk to the business. The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\\nRevenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\\nStrong profitability supports a robust capital structure. As of its January 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio is approximately 3.9x. The debt-to-equity ratio (which compares total debt to shareholder equity) is under 0.1x, meaning the company carries very little debt. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) reached nearly $96.7 billion for the fiscal year. This massive cash generation provides significant flexibility for research, development, and strategic acquisitions.\\nCredo faces intense competition from larger, well-established semiconductor companies and potential new entrants in the data infrastructure market. The company depends heavily on a small number of customers, making it vulnerable to losses or shifts in purchasing patterns. Its business model relies on third-party manufacturing partners, exposing the company to supply chain disruptions and geopolitical risks regarding operations in Taiwan and mainland China. Furthermore, evolving international trade controls and export restrictions could restrict its ability to serve key markets.\\nNvidia faces significant risks from global geopolitical tensions and export control regulations, particularly for high-performance AI chips. These restrictions have impacted sales to China and could continue to limit market access. The company faces intense competition from other hardware suppliers and internal development efforts by customers like Microsoft ( MSFT +1.22% ) , Amazon ( AMZN +0.51% ) , and Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.61% ) ( GOOG -0.77% ) . Nvidia also manages various legal challenges, including inquiries from competition regulators worldwide regarding its market practices.\\nNvidia trades at a lower forward P/E based on future earnings estimates, while both carry a high P/S ratio compared to historical averages.\\nI'd buy Nvidia, and it isn't close. Here's why:\\nCredo's growth is not the issue. Revenue in its latest quarter more than doubled from a year earlier, and management guided the current quarter higher still. The stock fell anyway because gross margin guidance came in flat, and shares now sit well below their 52-week high. That's the problem with a company that gets about 90% of its revenue from 10 customers. Any hint of slower spending from one of them moves the stock, and the valuation leaves no room for a miss.\\nNvidia is growing nearly as fast at a scale that makes Credo look like a rounding error. Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all. Nvidia has its own concentration risk, with two customers making up more than a third of sales, and the Hugging Face deal shows it's willing to spend to stay ahead.\\nFor a long-term investor, Nvidia offers the same AI infrastructure exposure at a lower price per dollar of expected earnings, with far less dependence on any single buyer. Credo belongs in a portfolio only as a small position you can afford to watch swing.\\nCredo more than tripled revenue last year but leans on 10 customers for 90% of sales, while Nvidia is doubling revenue at a scale nobody else in AI hardware can match.\\nBy Mike Schwenk – Updated Sep 25, 2026 at 3:00PM EST\\nCredo makes the high-speed connectivity chips and cables that move data between servers inside AI data centers, and revenue more than tripled in fiscal 2026.\\nNvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\\nBoth companies depend on a small group of customers, but Nvidia's scale, margins, and lower valuation make it the stronger buy today.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": \"article O-6 (outside the 40)\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-27\",\n  \"publisher\": \"bloomberg.com\",\n  \"title\": \"China May Let Alibaba Buy New Nvidia Chips, The Information Says\",\n  \"text\": \"(Bloomberg) -- The Chinese government has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd to purchase Nvidia Corp.'s new RTX Pro 5500 chips, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter.\\nEllison Pledges $9.2 Billion More in Oracle Shares for Loans\\nMcDonald's Selloff Hits 30% as Big Mac Inflation Spurs Pushback\\nThe Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked some companies to report on their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processor chip, which was released this month, including how many of them and what they would use the product for, the report said, adding that the ministry told some of the firms it intends to approve the purchases.\\nNvidia has faced export restrictions from the US as well as curbs from Beijing over the sale of its chips to China, due to a web of bilateral tensions ranging from security to competition on artificial intelligence.\\nThe US administration recently lifted restrictions on some Nvidia chips, though the products tend to be older-generation chips that have been sold in small quantities.\\nGLP-1 Users Are Furious After Employers Drop Insurance Benefits\\nThis $2 Billion Company Dresses HBO's The Pitt Cast and Real Doctors Too\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": \"article O-7 (outside the 40)\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n  \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\",\n  \"title\": \"NVIDIA Corporation (NVDA) Is a Trending Stock: Facts to Know Before Betting on It\",\n  \"text\": \"Shares of this maker of graphics chips for gaming and artificial intelligence have returned +9.1% over the past month versus the Zacks S&P 500 composite's +1.3% change. The Zacks Semiconductor - General industry, to which Nvidia belongs, has gained 8% over this period. Now the key question is: Where could the stock be headed in the near term?\\nAlthough media reports or rumors about a significant change in a company's business prospects usually cause its stock to trend and lead to an immediate price change, there are always certain fundamental factors that ultimately drive the buy-and-hold decision.\\nRather than focusing on anything else, we at Zacks prioritize evaluating the change in a company's earnings projection. This is because we believe the fair value for its stock is determined by the present value of its future stream of earnings.\\nOur analysis is essentially based on how sell-side analysts covering the stock are revising their earnings estimates to take the latest business trends into account. When earnings estimates for a company go up, the fair value for its stock goes up as well. And when a stock's fair value is higher than its current market price, investors tend to buy the stock, resulting in its price moving upward. Because of this, empirical studies indicate a strong correlation between trends in earnings estimate revisions and short-term stock price movements.\\nFor the current quarter, Nvidia is expected to post earnings of $2.47 per share, indicating a change of +90% from the year-ago quarter. The Zacks Consensus Estimate has changed +5.8% over the last 30 days.\\nFor the current fiscal year, the consensus earnings estimate of $9.25 points to a change of +93.9% from the prior year. Over the last 30 days, this estimate has changed +5.1%.\\nFor the next fiscal year, the consensus earnings estimate of $15.33 indicates a change of +65.8% from what Nvidia is expected to report a year ago. Over the past month, the estimate has changed +21.8%.\\nWhile earnings growth is arguably the most superior indicator of a company's financial health, nothing happens as such if a business isn't able to grow its revenues. After all, it's nearly impossible for a company to increase its earnings for an extended period without increasing its revenues. So, it's important to know a company's potential revenue growth.\\nFor Nvidia, the consensus sales estimate for the current quarter of $109.02 billion indicates a year-over-year change of +91.2%. For the current and next fiscal years, $406.05 billion and $671.95 billion estimates indicate +88% and +65.5% changes, respectively.\\nNo investment decision can be efficient without considering a stock's valuation. Whether a stock's current price rightly reflects the intrinsic value of the underlying business and the company's growth prospects is an essential determinant of its future price performance.\\nWhile comparing the current values of a company's valuation multiples, such as price-to-earnings (P/E), price-to-sales (P/S), and price-to-cash flow (P/CF), with its own historical values helps determine whether its stock is fairly valued, overvalued, or undervalued, comparing the company relative to its peers on these parameters gives a good sense of the reasonability of the stock's price.\\nThe Zacks Value Style Score (part of the Zacks Style Scores system), which pays close attention to both traditional and unconventional valuation metrics to grade stocks from A to F (an A is better than a B; a B is better than a C; and so on), is pretty helpful in identifying whether a stock is overvalued, rightly valued, or temporarily undervalued.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": \"article O-8 (outside the 40)\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n  \"publisher\": \"gurufocus.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia Gains as Apple Pulls AI Off the Meter\",\n  \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ), the AI-accelerator and data-center platform leader, faced a fresh edge-computing test as Apple ( NASDAQ:AAPL ) pushed its new Macs as machines capable of running heavyweight AI privately instead of constantly calling the cloud. Nvidia shares gained approximately 0.7% to $229.08, suggesting investors were hardly treating Apple's move as an immediate threat to the GPU giant's core data-center engine.\\nWarning! GuruFocus has detected 3 Warning Sign with MSFT.\\nApple showed four Mac Studios working together to run a trillion-parameter model, using shared memory across the machines to tackle workloads once associated almost entirely with expensive server infrastructure. That is an eye-catching demonstration, but the scale gap remains enormous: Nvidia's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion overall, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI. The GuruFocus picture reinforces that underlying strength, with Nvidia carrying a GF Score of 95/100 and particularly strong readings for profitability, growth and financial strength, while GF Value remains the noticeably weaker part of the profile.\\nThe bigger question is not whether a handful of Macs can run a giant model. They clearly can. The question is how much inference, coding and repetitive enterprise AI work permanently shifts from cloud infrastructure onto local machines. Apple could chip away at some private and predictable workloads, especially where data security and recurring token costs matter. But hyperscale training, massive networking demands and unpredictable peak workloads still play directly into Nvidia's territory. For Nvidia investors, the real risk starts only if local AI becomes a structural substitute for cloud inference rather than another layer of the computing stack.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": \"article O-9 (outside the 40)\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n  \"publisher\": \"barchart.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock is Historically Cheap. The Decade-Low Multiple Reflects Risks, But This Looks Like a Can't-Miss Buy Opportunity for NVDA.\",\n  \"text\": \"Nvidia Stock is Historically Cheap. The Decade-Low Multiple Reflects Risks, But This Looks Like a Can't-Miss Buy Opportunity for NVDA.\\nNvidia (NVDA) stock is currently trading at less than 17 times expected forward 12-month earnings, representing the cheapest valuation the shares have commanded in over a decade and roughly half the multiple investors were willing to pay as recently as early 2025.\\nThis dramatic de-rating has occurred against a backdrop of accelerating, not decelerating, fundamentals — a paradox that sits at the heart of the debate over whether NVDA's compressed multiple signals heightened risk or a rare value buying opportunity.\\nSpaceX Has Huge, Unusual Put Options Volume - Is SPCX Set to Fall or Rise From Here?\\nLucid Just Struck a Partnership With Bolt on Mobility. What This Means for LCID Stock.\\nThe disconnect between Nvidia's soaring earnings power and its shrinking valuation multiple suggests the market is pricing in meaningful skepticism about the sustainability of the current AI spending cycle.\\nThe fundamental case for Nvidia remains extraordinarily strong. Fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue surging 117% to $89 billion, and management has guided for approximately $108 billion in third-quarter revenue, implying 89% growth.\\nCEO Jensen Huang has projected that chip sales could double in calendar year 2027, while CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled. There has never been a company of Nvidia's scale growing at anything close to this rate.\\nYet several legitimate risks explain the market's caution. Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\\nOther tangible headwinds include calls from leading artificial intelligence (AI) labs, including Anthropic and OpenAI, to slow frontier development; rising community backlash against data center construction; soaring interest rates that pressure hyperscaler capital budgets; and Nvidia's complete exclusion from the Chinese data center market.\\nAdditionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\\nThe valuation math, however, is difficult to ignore. At 14 times next fiscal year's consensus earnings estimate, Nvidia trades at a fraction of its 10-year average price/earnings (P/E) ratio of 61 – and well below semiconductor peers like Advanced Micro Devices (AMD) at over 200 times trailing earnings and Broadcom (AVGO) at 46 times.\\nAnalysts remain overwhelmingly bullish, with 45 of 50 firms rating the stock a \\\"Strong Buy,\\\" a mean price target near $326 implying 42% upside from current levels, and earnings estimates that have been revised sharply higher — from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029 — over just the past nine months.\\nIf Nvidia simply maintains its current trailing P/E of 28 while delivering on consensus earnings projections, the stock would roughly double over the next 12 to 18 months.\\nThe decade-low multiple is not so much a red flag as it is the market's attempt to reconcile unprecedented growth with unprecedented uncertainty about how long that growth can last. For investors with a multi-year horizon and a tolerance for volatility tied to AI spending cycles, the risk-reward profile at current levels tilts decisively toward opportunity rather than warning.\\nThis article was created with the support of automated content tools from our partners at Sigma.AI. Together, our financial data and AI solutions help us to deliver more informed market headline analysis to readers faster than ever.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": \"article O-10 (outside the 40)\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n  \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia's Demand Is Outpacing Its 70% Growth Forecast, and That Is Just the Beginning\",\n  \"text\": \"There may be no better company to represent the AI boom than Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA). Its graphics processing units (GPUs) are the backbone of artificial intelligence data centers, and in just three years, the company's market cap has skyrocketed by 445% to $5.5 trillion, making it the most valuable company in the world .\\nWhat continues to impress many investors is that Nvidia isn't done growing yet. In its fiscal 2027 second-quarter report, Management forecast that in fiscal 2028, its sales will rise by 70%, thanks to continued demand for AI chips.\\nBut what's really interesting is that Nvidia leadership said it could double its sales in fiscal 2028 if it were able to overcome the constraints on the supply of commodities needed to produce its hardware.\\nIn short, demand for Nvidia's chips is far outpacing the company's own forecast sales grown. Here's what's happening and why it's good news for Nvidia investors.\\nOn Nvidia's second-quarter fiscal 2027 earnings call , Nvidia CFO Colette Kress said:\\nIncredibly, we are seeing demand acceleration even at our scale. Customer forecasts point to our growth doubling next year. However, as I mentioned earlier, we expect to grow approximately 70% as we are supply-constrained.\\nKress is absolutely right. This level of sales growth is incredible for a $5.5 trillion company. To put it in perspective, Apple 's market cap is just under $5 trillion, and its revenue growth was 16% in the third quarter to $109 billion. Still impressive for its size, but nowhere near Nvidia's growth.\\nThere are a handful of reasons why demand for artificial intelligence processors remains high, the first of which is that agentic AI is replacing human-generated prompts as the main driver of workload growth. In short, AI is doing more tasks on its own, and that's driving computing needs much higher.\\nNvidia CEO Jensen Huang said on the earnings call that \\\"the amount of compute necessary is just extraordinary\\\" because AI agents use between 15 and 100 times as much compute as would be required with a human guiding the work. All of this is combining to boost demand for Nvidia's GPUs.\\nHuang's not the only one projecting far more spending on AI infrastructure. S&P Global recently forecast that tech companies will spend an estimated $1.3 trillion on AI next year.\\nSometimes it can be a problem when a company can't meet its customers' demand. But in this case, it's not so much of a problem because the entire tech industry -- including nearly all hardware makers -- is struggling to keep up.\\nFor example, Micron Technology 's memory chips are having a significant problem, with demand far outpacing supply. That's led to skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron, and global supply constraints.\\nThe big picture for Nvidia investors is that agentic AI could drive GPU demand much higher than it is now and will likely continue to boost Nvidia's sales for years to come. As Huang noted on the call:\\nToday, the vast majority of AI is prompted by people. I believe that this last month, it has crossed. Most AI are now agentic. But in the future, every company will have a whole bunch of agents.\\nNvidia says it's working to increase supply to meet customer demand. If it succeeds, it could double its sales in fiscal 2028. But even if it doesn't, Nvidia appears to be on the cusp of another surge in processor demand that could last for years.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": \"article O-11 (outside the 40)\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n  \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Marvell vs. NVIDIA: One AI Stock Looks Like the Better Buy Now\",\n  \"text\": \"Both Marvell Technology, Inc. MRVL and its larger counterpart, NVIDIA Corporation NVDA, are benefiting from the rising Data Center demand amid the ongoing artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure boom. While both companies delivered strong quarterly results, differences in profitability, AI exposure, and outlook warrant a closer look when determining which stock to buy now.\\nMarvell's AI Momentum Powers Record Growth\\nGiven Marvell's scale, the company posted substantial revenue growth in its latest quarterly results. Revenues for the fiscal second quarter of 2027 reached a record $2.739 billion, up 37% year over year, according to the company's Aug. 27 press release. With Marvell supplying vital infrastructure for data center systems, its Data Center revenues grew even faster, up 46% year over year.\\nFor the fiscal third quarter of 2027, management expects revenues to reach $3.15 billion, plus or minus 5%. At the midpoint, it represents roughly 15% sequential growth. Management also remained optimistic about revenue growth throughout fiscal year 2027 and 2028. This suggests that Marvell's strong performance goes beyond the latest quarter, with management increasing its forward expectations.\\nMarvell's Chairman and chief executive officer, Matt Murphy, said the company is seeing exceptionally strong AI-related bookings, with growth expected to accelerate further across their Data Center portfolio, supported by robust demand in Connectivity and accelerating Custom business.\\nNVIDIA's AI Boom Fuels Record Growth and Strong Profitability\\nNVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release. Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\\nNVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\\nNVIDIA expects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, for the fiscal third quarter of 2027, indicating continued sequential growth. Profitability is also expected to remain strong, while the company's Vera Rubin platform is now in full production, positioning NVIDIA to capitalize on continued AI infrastructure demand.\\nBanking on AI and Data Center demand, both Marvell and NVIDIA delivered strong quarterly results. Still, their exposure to the AI opportunity varies significantly.\\nMarvell's growth is increasingly driven by strong data center demand. However, if capital spending by AI infrastructure companies slows down in the future, Marvell's growth could decelerate. In contrast, NVIDIA's exposure across the broader AI computing platform, along with significantly faster Data Center growth, provides greater diversification within the AI market and reduces the company's exposure to concentration risk.\\nMarvell also appears to be emphasizing revenue expansion, but its forecasted fiscal third-quarter 2027 non-GAAP gross margin of 57.5-58.5% is below the reported 58.9% in the fiscal second quarter. This suggests that the expected revenue increase may not translate into proportional earnings, a concern for investors to monitor. In comparison, NVIDIA expects a non-GAAP gross margin of 74% in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, highlighting its much stronger margin profile.\\nMoreover, NVIDIA appears more efficient than Marvell at generating profits from shareholders' equity. This is because NVIDIA's return on equity (ROE) of 96% exceeds Marvell's ROE of 17.9%.\\nTherefore, NVIDIA's stronger growth, profitability, and ROE make it a more compelling investment option now than Marvell. Additionally, NVIDIA appears more attractively valued than Marvell. Per the price/earnings ratio, NVDA trades at 24.39 forward earnings compared with MRVL's forward earnings multiple of 62.25.\\nNVIDIA currently has a Zacks Rank #1 (Strong Buy), while Marvell has a Zacks Rank #3 (Hold). You can see the complete list of today's Zacks Rank #1 stocks here .\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": \"article O-12 (outside the 40)\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n  \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n  \"title\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\",\n  \"text\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\\nNVIDIA (NVDA) stock trades near $225, about 28.3 times its adjusted earnings over the past twelve months. Adjusted here means normalized net income with stock-based compensation added back, the same broad basis analysts use in their forecasts. For a stock near its 52-week high, that looks steep. But the multiple leaves out the growth analysts already expect, and that growth depends on how much NVIDIA can supply.\\nNVIDIA Looks Ordinary Once The Forecast Earnings Arrive\\nOn the earnings analysts expect for fiscal 2027, the same price is about 24.1 times. On what they expect for fiscal 2028, it is about 14.5 times. The price is the same. The earnings analysts expect it to buy are not.\\nNVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell. Production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already in from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\\nSo What Stands Between NVIDIA And Those Earnings?\\nFor fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there. The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme.\\nSome of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\\nAnalysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\\nAnd NVIDIA’s Gross Margin Is Guided Lower As Memory Costs Climb\\nConsensus has earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, so analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady. Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\nFor the forecast to hold, that lost gross margin has to be made up further down the income statement. That is the second condition behind the fiscal 2028 multiple, alongside supply.\\nSo Is NVIDIA Cheap, Or Only If The Chips Get Built?\\nOnly if the chips get built, which means the supply arrives and the margins hold. The trailing multiple cannot tell you either. How much would you stake on a year that has not started?\\nChecking that for every stock you own is a job most people have no time for. Since its inception, our rule-based Trefis High Quality Portfolio has outperformed its benchmark, a blend of three major indices.\\nOr hunt for these yourself: our Forward Valuation Discount screen ranks stocks priced furthest below their expected earnings. It shows where to look, and the forecast still has to come true.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": \"article O-13 (outside the 40)\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n  \"publisher\": \"cloudwars.com\",\n  \"title\": \"AWS and NVIDIA Scale AI Infrastructure With 2 Million Additional GPUs\",\n  \"text\": \"AI and Copilots Welcome to the Acceleration Economy AI Index, a weekly segment where we cover the most important recent news in AI innovation, funding, and solutions in under 10 minutes. Our goal is to get you up to speed – the same speed AI innovation is taking place nowadays – and prepare you for that upcoming customer call, board meeting, or conversation with your colleague.\\nCybersecurity The practice of defending computers, servers, mobile devices, electronic systems, networks, and data from malicious attacks.\\nWhen it comes to AI infrastructure , AWS and NVIDIA are somewhat of a power couple. The two companies have a longstanding partnership dating back 16 years, focused on delivering NVIDIA-accelerated computing through AWS.\\nNow, the two powerhouses have announced a staggering expansion of this existing partnership which will, among other things — but this is certainly the standout headline — include the deployment of 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across AWS infrastructure between 2027 and 2028. The aim, according to Amazon, is to meet surging demand for AI infrastructure “from frontier labs, global enterprises, startups, and governments.”\\nThe 2 million additional GPUs, building on the more than 1 million NVIDIA GPUs AWS has previously committed to deploying beginning in 2026, will include NVIDIA’s Blackwell Ultra, Rubin, and Rubin Ultra architectures. They’ll be deployed alongside NVIDIA Vera CPUs and other technologies, creating a powerful foundation for next-generation AI use cases.\\n“Customers want the freedom to choose the best tools for their AI workloads, and they want confidence that everything works seamlessly together,” said Matt Garman, CEO of AWS. “That’s why we’ve invested deeply with NVIDIA to make AWS the best place to run NVIDIA AI technologies, optimizing performance across our infrastructure from networking and security to deployment.\\n“This expanded collaboration gives frontier labs, enterprises, and governments even more ways to build and deploy AI on AWS.”\\nThis push is being driven by growing demand for next-generation AI workloads, particularly agentic AI and physical AI. On the physical AI side, Amazon Robotics will work with NVIDIA to integrate its full-stack physical AI platform, helping ramp up the development of Amazon’s robotics projects. The broader NVIDIA infrastructure, including that mind-boggling deployment of GPUs , will provide the compute and tech needed to run these projects at scale.\\nI mentioned at the start of this article that the expanded partnership between AWS and NVIDIA had more to it than just the headline-grabbing “2 million additional GPUs.” One of the most significant parts of the announcement is what the two companies intend to do together in the AI factory space.\\nAWS and NVIDIA will build AI factories for the U.S. government, enabling the delivery of “100,000 GPUs on AWS’s secure infrastructure for federal and national-security workloads.” This puts AWS in a very strong position in one of the most competitive and potentially lucrative areas of the AI market.\\nWhat this expanded partnership demonstrates is the realization of what has been discussed among governments, businesses, and other organizations for years now: that demand for AI is going to continue growing rapidly. AWS and NVIDIA are preparing for an AI market that will require a level of infrastructure that simply doesn’t exist today, because it hasn’t been necessary for current workloads.\\nFor AWS in particular, this expanded partnership strengthens its position as a major player for organizations looking to build and deploy AI at scale. For NVIDIA, while neither company has disclosed the financial value of the expanded partnership, the value of the GPUs alone could represent tens of billions of dollars in revenue.\\nFor the industry, and in particular for customers that want to scale at pace, this latest development could really matter as demand for AI compute continues to accelerate.\\nKieron Allen is a Cloud Wars Analyst examining innovations in, and the future impact of, the latest AI, cloud, cybersecurity, and data technology developments. In his ongoing analyses and video reports, Allen focuses on the platforms, applications, people, and ideas that will mold our digital future. After serving as the Online Editor for BBC Sky at Night Magazine and as the Editorial Assistant for BBC Focus Magazine, Kieron became a freelance journalist in 2015 where his focus on the business technology market became a key passion. Kieron partners with technology start-ups and organizations that share his interests in science, social affairs, non-profit work, fashion and the arts.\\nLost your password? Please enter your username or email address. You will receive a link to create a new password via email.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": \"article O-14 (outside the 40)\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-26\",\n  \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia Is Weighing a $10 Billion Stake in Anthropic's IPO. It Would Be Buying Its Own Demand.\",\n  \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) might be gearing up to write Anthropic a second $10 billion check.\\nThe chipmaker's in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering (IPO) , Reuters said this month, a deal that could deepen its ties to a major customer. A source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion.\\nThe artificial intelligence (AI) company behind the Claude models is reportedly looking to raise as much as $100 billion in a listing that might value it at roughly $2 trillion. Reuters has since said that the IPO may slip past the U.S. midterm elections in November. Of course, the plans might still change, and neither company has confirmed the talks.\\nWhat would Nvidia get for the money?\\nTwo $10 billion checks\\nThe first check was directly tied to Nvidia's hardware. In November 2025, Nvidia said it would invest up to $10 billion in Anthropic, along with up to $5 billion from Microsoft . Anthropic, in turn, committed to buy $30 billion in Azure computing capacity. And its first deal with Nvidia covered up to 1 gigawatt of compute on Nvidia's Grace Blackwell and Vera Rubin systems.\\nLater, in March, CEO Jensen Huang said the $10 billion Anthropic investment would probably be Nvidia's last in the company. His reason for a similar call on OpenAI was that it was \\\"going to go public.\\\"\\nThe check would buy a sliver of Anthropic\\nAt a $2 trillion valuation , $10 billion is around half of 1% of the company. That's a big chunk of the offering (a tenth of a $100 billion raise), but a small slice of the business.\\nNvidia's done this before. It was one of 10 cornerstone investors in chip designer Arm Holdings' 2023 IPO. Together, the group indicated interest in buying up to $735 million in stock at the IPO price and on the same terms as other buyers.\\nIf Anthropic's deal works like that, Nvidia's cash would buy the same stock, at the same price, as everybody else. The main bonus, I think, is the signal. An early pledge from a backer this big might reassure other buyers.\\nBut the reported talks don't involve anything like 2025's hardware commitment, at least so far. And Anthropic has been spreading its compute dollars around. It's lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon , and 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google. It's also building a team to design its own chips, while saying it'll still use several suppliers' hardware, Nvidia's included.\\nAnthropic said money from its latest funding round would partly go toward expanding compute to meet demand for Claude. Money from an IPO might go the same way, and some could come back to Nvidia through the Nvidia-based computing capacity Anthropic pays for.\\nSo I think Nvidia would be buying its own demand -- and paying to stay close to a customer that's buying from rivals, too.\\nFor Nvidia, $10 billion is a small sum. Over the three months ended July 26 (its fiscal 2027 second quarter), it earned $59.7 billion, more than twice its year-ago profit. And revenue growth accelerated to 106% year over year, up from the previous quarter's 85% rate.\\nChecks like this are adding up even faster. Nvidia carried its stakes in private companies on its books at $47.9 billion as of July 26. That compares to $22.3 billion in late January, when its fiscal year started, and just $3.8 billion a year earlier. Including public holdings, the company's equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\\nIn other words, its private stakes rose over twelvefold in a year when quarterly revenue about doubled.\\nSo Nvidia's investment gains and its sales now depend on the same thing: AI spending. If that spending cooled, Nvidia could face smaller orders and markdowns on its stakes at the same time.\\nIn the end, I don't think a $10 billion check changes much about Nvidia by itself. It's around a sixth of a quarter's profit, and keeping a major customer close is arguably money well spent.\\nAt about $225 as I write, shares cost about 14 times expected earnings for fiscal 2028 -- a fair price for a company growing so fast, in my view.\\nI'd just watch how big that investment portfolio gets.\\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 26, 2026 at 3:37PM EST\\nIn November 2025, Nvidia agreed to invest up to $10 billion in Anthropic as part of a deal linked to Nvidia-powered computing capacity.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": \"article O-15 (outside the 40)\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-27\",\n  \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n  \"title\": \"China May Reopen Nvidia's AI Market. How Will NVDA Stock React Monday?\",\n  \"text\": \"China May Reopen Nvidia's AI Market. How Will NVDA Stock React Monday?\\nChina may allow Alibaba and ByteDance to buy a new Nvidia chip, The Information reported on Sunday. NVDA stock closed Friday at $225.07 and gets its first chance to react when Wall Street opens Monday.\\nThe chip is a powerful card for professional computers. It is not the data-center hardware Nvidia once sold to China in bulk.\\nChina's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT), which oversees the tech sector, asked the two firms how many RTX PRO 5500 cards they want and why. That is according to The Information, citing two people familiar with the matter.\\nChinese AI firms are short of computing power for their chatbots and agents, the report said. Neither Beijing nor the companies has confirmed the plan.\\nNvidia markets the card for AI agents, AI inference and simulation. It carries 84 GB of memory and draws up to 600 watts. Because it is a workstation part, it sits outside the data-center chips targeted by US export curbs.\\nHow NVDA Stock Reacted to China Chip News Before\\nNvidia's $108 billion forecast for the current quarter assumes no China data-center chip revenue, the company says. Any sales there would come on top of that outlook.\\nThe last big China headline hit the other way. In September 2025, Beijing told firms to stop buying Nvidia's RTX Pro 6000D, and the stock fell 2.6% that day.\\n\\\"I think that we could only be in service of a market if the country wants us to be,\\\" Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said at the time.\\nSunday's report lands days after President Donald Trump hosted Xi Jinping in Washington. US Trade Representative Jamieson Greer said national security export controls were taken \\\"off the table,\\\" the Korea Times reported.\\nMeanwhile, Huawei and Cambricon hold nearly 80% of China's AI server market, analysts cited by the outlet said. Local rivals keep growing, as Enflame's Shanghai debut showed this month.\\nWall Street already leans bullish. All 31 analysts tracked by TipRanks rate the stock a buy, with an average target of $324.32.\\nThe report did not say how many cards China would allow or when. It also gave no sign that bigger data-center chips would follow. Nvidia shares have swung on China news before, including after Trump's call with Huang this month.\\nRead the Original story China May Reopen Nvidia's AI Market. How Will NVDA Stock React Monday? by Lockridge Okoth at beincrypto.com\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": \"article 31 of the packet\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-27\",\n  \"publisher\": \"ca.finance.yahoo.com\",\n  \"title\": \"China chipmaking stocks tumble as Beijing reportedly mulls allowing Nvidia sales\",\n  \"text\": \"Investing.com-- Chinese chipmaking stocks fell sharply on Monday after a report said Beijing was considering allowing some local firms to buy NVIDIA Corp's (NASDAQ:NVDA) advanced chips.\\nThe Information reported that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked domestic firms, including Alibaba and ByteDance, to report how many Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 units they planned to purchase and what they would use them for.\\nThe questioning signaled an intent to eventually approve the sales, The Information reported.\\nSemiconductor Manufacturing International Corp (HK:0981), China's biggest chipmaker by volume, slid 3.7%, while rival Hua Hong Semiconductor Ltd (HK:1347) shed nearly 5%.\\nMoore Threads Technology Co Ltd (SS:688795), regarded by many as China's answer to Nvidia, slid 6.3%, while Cambricon Technologies Corp Ltd (SS:688256) fell 5.7%.\\nChipmaking equipment maker NAURA Technology Group Co Ltd (SZ:002371) lost 3.4%, while memory chip major CXMT Corp (SS:688825) fell nearly 4%. Chipmakers lagged broader gains in Chinese and Hong Kong markets.\\nThe RTX Pro 5500 is a Blackwell-generation professional workstation GPU released by Nvidia earlier this month. The chip, while capable of heavy AI computing tasks, still sits outside the AI accelerator category of chips the U.S. has largely blocked from being sold to China.\\nThe Information report comes after the U.S. and China held a summit in Washington and agreed to better trade relations, including a $30 billion exemption on tariffs both ways.\\nBut the two did not discuss AI chip sales.\\nThe approval of Nvidia chip sales in China presents heightened competition for local chipmakers, who are racing to produce comparable processors amid a storm of regulatory headwinds. China's homegrown chipmaking processes are still seen as years behind the advanced technology found in the U.S. and Taiwan.\\nBeijing was seen leaning heavily into developing more homegrown AI chips, with several major and upcoming players– including CXMT and Moore Threads– logging bumper stock market debuts over the past year.\\nVerbund shares rise 3% after Deutsche Bank upgrade to Hold\\nFTSE 100 today: Stocks edge up as Iran diplomacy hopes persist\"\n }\n]","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from bs4 import BeautifulSoup; p=\\\"docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\\\"; s=BeautifulSoup(open(p).read(),\\\"html.parser\\\"); print(s.get_text(\\\"\\\\n\\\",strip=True))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from bs4 import BeautifulSoup; p=\\\"docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\\\"; s=BeautifulSoup(open(p).read(),\\\"html.parser\\\"); print(s.get_text(\\\"\\\\n\\\",strip=True))'\"","aggregated_output":"Hivemind 抽取与开题标准\n目录\n✓\n已拍的 12 条\n1\n用词\n2\n账本记什么\n3\n问题的五类\n4\n开题标准\n5\n证据标准\n6\n分量\n7\n研究观点\n8\n金标\n9\n改提示词、测量、迭代\n10\nAlex 拍过的\n11\n冲突怎么摆平\n12\n校验和出处\nHivemind · 抽取这一步的 PRD · 第 5 版（第 3 版已签；D248 按 Alex 逐个选的七条修订；D250 证据的四条界线和计数单位）\n抽取与开题标准\n已签\n生效。\n2026-10-29 Alex 拍了 12 条（D243）；2026-10-30 他逐个选了颗粒度的七条（D248，见\"已拍\"一节），改了第 2、3、4 节，升为第 4 版；2026-10-31 他按我给的倾向定了证据的四条界线和计数单位（D250，见\"已拍\"一节），改了第 5、8、9 节，升为第 5 版。这一页取代第 1 版（\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-analyst-standard/standard.md\n，D229），是抽取这一步唯一的尺子：提示词、金标、回归测量都从它推出。改它要先在\ndocs/DECISIONS.md\n记下 Alex 的原话，再改这一页，再做一次只读的漂移检查。\n2026-10-29 · D241、D242、D243\n分支 fix/extraction-quality-2026-10-28\n起草过程和校验：\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/\n读法：每条规则后面的灰色小字是它的出处。标了出处的例子是判例或原文里的例子；没标的是按那条规则举的，帮助理解，不是判例。没有出处的只有\"我定的记录约定\"。\n证据\n一个在追这家公司的分析员读到会记进本周笔记的陈述，就留；留下以后另判分量\n第 1 版 §2、§4\n。整体倾向谨慎：杂音很大，不是什么都要抽，不是什么都算线索；但新信息带来的增量要敏感，不能漏\nD243\n。预测、传闻、反证、远期、条件情景都可以是证据，不因为\"还没发生\"\"不是官方\"\"离现在远\"\"和我们方向相反\"就不留\n第 1 版 §2\n。有名有姓的动作缺金额、缺日期也照留，分量可以低一点\nD240\n。泛泛的主张和背景是常识，没有数字和具体的叙事就不收\nD243\n。\n开题\n一个问题值得单独追，当它的答案可能改变仓位、能在一个日期用公开材料核对、而且库里没有问题在问同一件事\n第 1 版 §6\n。\n研究观点\n一个观点是关于业务和世界的、有论证的看法，它的立场来自论证和市场已经定价的东西相比\nAGENTS §8\n。问题当前落在哪一档，由判断这一步按 PRD 06 和 D198 读证据推出。\n一张图：同一篇文章里每句话的去向\n一句话\n在第 5.3 节的不留清单上\n→ 先看那一行的\"边界\"一列：写着\"不能用这一行删掉\"或\"按 5.1 判\"的，按第 5.1 节判，留下的走下面；写着\"要留\"或\"按本来分量\"的照办；写着\"可选\"的归可选；其余不留\n分析员会记\n→ 留为证据\n第 5 节\n记来源层：事实、当事人陈述、第三方观点、传闻\n进账本 · 第 5.5 节\n分量：核心、辅助、可选\n只在评估时判，不进账本 · 第 6 节\n归档：挂到最窄的那个已开问题\nD207\n；没有问题接，记 no home 扔掉，评估时逐条读\nD209\n这篇文章还提出了值得追的新问题吗？\n第 4 节\n过了测试，属于五类之一\n→ 候选问题；和库里已有问题是不是重复，由匹配判\nD211、D190\n没过\n→ 不开，它只是证据\n已拍\nAlex 拍板的 12 条\n2026-10-29 Alex 签字（D243）。第 5 条他定得比我的倾向更严；其余 11 条按倾向：\"其他几条的意见我都ok，sign off\"。下表每条写定下的是什么、落在哪一节。\n第 5 条，Alex 原话：\"这种典型的主张背景属于常识，基本上抽出来也没有太多的意义除非带数字和具体的叙事不然都是属于不采纳\"\n整体方向，Alex 原话：\"不是什么事情都是惊弓之鸟需要抽取，杂音还是很大的，我们整体应该倾向谨慎不要看什么都是线索的感觉，但是同时我们要敏感一些新的信息带来的增量不要错过\"\n#\n定下的\n在哪一节\n1\n分量按信息量定档：核心要碰到关键判断，并带一个量、一个确定状态或一个带期间的预测；别家公司自己的经营数字、对公司是小事的，算辅助；预测离现在越远，越可能只是辅助，由判断定。缺金额、缺日期只影响分到哪一档，不是不留的理由\n第 6 节\n2\n\"共同量\"按 D191 落实：任何一家公司之外、好几家都会受它影响的量或价格（合计支出、合约价、利率）都可以开题，开题理由叫\nshared_quantity\n；期间按自然年。提示词和代码要跟着改\n第 3 节 D 类\n3\n生态数字（论文数、获奖、下载量、开发者人数、大会人数）可选\n第 6 节\n4\n在研公司的估值倍数、市值、孤零零的目标价可选；带推导的条件估值仍是辅助\n第 5.3、6 节\n5\n典型的主张和背景是常识，没有数字和具体的叙事不收；不是常识的新信息，照第 5.1 节的规则判\n第 5.1、5.3 节\n6\n旧事重提：重提的过去实际数可选；重提的季度指引和仍然有效的预测、计划按本来分量；文字或日期看不出是旧事的不算重提\n第 5.3、6 节\n7\n收购算约束性义务（写明规模、尚未完成）\n第 3 节 C 类\n8\n改提示词时的回归判法：全部文章各发一次和上一版比，\"别的版本都留住、只有这一版丢了\"的核心或辅助才算回归信号；它是诊断，验收仍是三次发送\n第 9.3 节\n9\n来源层：一句话符合两行时取表里上面那行；混合的一条按主要说法定层，次要部分写进摘要\n第 5.5 节\n10\n只有形容的引语不收\n第 5.3 节\n11\n金标冻结后发现错：另记一份带理由的更正，写明改前改后，不覆盖原文件；测量用更正后的版本并注明\n第 8 节\n12\n达标线里\"垃圾\"一项（每篇留下的无关陈述）用定稿金标重算，Luna 基线也重算\n第 9.2 节\nD248：颗粒度的七条（2026-10-30）\nAlex 逐个听了例子、逐个选的，七个都选 A；改了第 2、3、4 节，标准升为第 4 版，金标要按第 4 版重推：开题部分逐条重判，证据部分逐条核对（D244）。\n原则，Alex 原话：\"我觉得核心就是这个颗粒度的把控要符合一个高端的hedge fund analyst 的思维。不要搜集一堆鸡毛蒜皮的事情，要看重要的大事，合并整合关键的信息，这个是这个步骤的原则\"\n#\n定下的\n在哪一节\n1\n同一个计划的几种说法，最后核对同一个数的，是一题；各自会变、会分别盯的数才分开；属于已按金额开题的计划的一笔交易并进去\n第 4 节\n2\n同一件事的不同截止日：早的那题开着时，晚的截止日是它的证据；早的到期没有结果、事情还在追，系统开下一个截止日的续期题，带上证据，不等新文章\n第 4 节\n3\n同一代产品的几个里程碑：一题，问能带来收入的那一步（通常是开始出货）；送样、发布作证据；会分别下注的里程碑（新药的三期数据和获批）各一题\n第 4 节\n4\n公司自己的量只按财年开题；按日历年的估计是财年那题的证据\n第 4 节\n5\n事件和政策题里模型写的期间起点，不拿来挡合并\n第 4 节\n6\n单笔交易、产品、项目，规模要大到能改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险才开题；够不上的不开题，它的陈述留不留照第 5 节判，留下的是证据\n第 3 节 C 类、第 4 节\n7\n原始说话人、原始日期、数字都相同的几条证据，代码认作一条，判断时只给看一次、写明几家报道，每条照存；不知道的说话人或日期不算相同\n第 2 节\nD250：证据的四条界线和计数单位（2026-10-31）\nv63 留下、金标判不收的 117 条交给没写金标也没写提示词的读者盲判，混着它分不出的对照：对照上读者和金标一致 93–95%，这 117 条上只有 70%。模型偏偏在标准没说清的地方留东西。五处各给了倾向，Alex 原话：\"五处都按你的倾向定，计数单位也改; 你要修正尺子；你已经多次因为尺子标准问题陷入假的测量\"。\n#\n定下的\n在哪一节\n1\n只描述一个现成安排（合作、客户在用、已有部署）、没有新动作的，是背景，不收，文中没写日期也一样；仍然有效的指引、预测、计划照旧按本来分量\n5.3\n2\n别家公司的事，说不出它通过需求、供给、竞争、定价这类路径动了在研公司哪个问题的，不收；只因同一篇文章、同一场活动或同一个生态挨着的，不收\n5.3\n3\n公司自己、伙伴或第三方发布的对比是产品细节，不收；只有对手拿来和本公司产品比的按 5.1 判（判例 4 不变）；同一句里伙伴、客户采用或集成的动作不因这一条删，照旧按 5.1 判\n5.3\n4\n具名分析师对一件已记下的事的解读，没有新事实或推算的，不收；有新事实或推算的按 5.1 判；条件估值不变\n5.3\n5\n计数单位：一条不收的金标条目，只有带着它的那条记录本身不是金标判为核心、辅助或可选的同一件事时，才算留下；引文里顺带带出的不收半句，是那件事的同一条记录\n9.2\n尺子跟着修：金标的证据部分按第 5 版重推、另存冻结，打分程序遇到证据部分版本对不上就拒绝打分；开题部分重推之前不报开题的分（第 8 节）。\n我定的记录约定\n只定怎么记，不改变留不留、开不开。\n页面杂项（广告、相关文章预告、推荐列表、别的新闻标题）一律归第 1 版的\"模板\"一行，不另起名字。\n标题里的说法和正文是同一条证据，不单记\n第 1 版 §2 同一事实的复述\n。\n分四类时看\"谁在说谁\"（第 5.1 节）。四类只是金标里的标签，不进账本，不影响留不留。\n金标里，不在不留清单上、按第 5.1 节判掉的句子标出来并写理由，供复核（第 5.3 节、第 8 节）。\n1\n这一页用的词：一词一义\n词\n意思\n不要和它混\n陈述\n原文里某一方说的一句话或一件事\n证据\n被留下的陈述，带引文、摘要、说话人、原始出处、来源层\n作者的观点（它本身可以是一条证据）\n问题（观点）\n账本里追踪的一件事：一个问题句、一组结果档、一个到期日，写下后不改\nPRD 06\n候选问题\n抽取这一步提议新开的问题\n标的问题、主题问题\n标的：答案是一家可交易公司（或币、商品）自己的经营结果。主题：答案是世界上的一件事，影响很多公司\nPRD 06\n开题理由\n候选问题值得开的几类理由之一，答案里写在\nopens_as\nD184；英文 admission reason\n结果档\n一个问题事先固定、互不重叠、覆盖全部结果的结局，五档或是非两档\nPRD 06；英文 rung\n分量\n来源层\n事实、当事人陈述、第三方观点、传闻\nPRD 06；英文 evidence tier\n分量\n分量\n一条证据对判断有多重要：核心、辅助、可选、不收\n第 6 节；D243\n。Alex 说的\"档位低一点\"指它\n结果档、来源层\n研究用途\n判断这一步引用一条证据去支持一个具体结论时，它撑得住多强：直接可用、有条件可用、线索、不可用\nD221，第 7 节\n分量\n七类驱动\n一条陈述为什么对公司业务重要的七条路径\nD208\n。需求：客户要买多少，如订单、客户支出、贷款需求、客流。供给与投入：能做出多少、成本多少，如产能、原料和零部件价格、燃料、矿石品位。竞争与份额：对手的产品、价格、开店、赢单。定价与装机：公司能收多少价、存量多大，如售价、费率、利差、装机量、存量客户。产品周期：发布、出货、试验、获批、延期。政策：法规、许可、关税、利率、监管决定。资本与义务：融资、担保、承诺、投资、收购\n问题类型\n市场预期与估值\n市场已经定价了什么：估值倍数、市值、目标价、股价反应。观点的立场要和它比\nAGENTS §8\n。对一个业务量的一致预期（收入、EPS）是那个业务量的证据，不归这里\n七类驱动\n研究组\n几个问题共同在追的一件正在进行的事\nD165\n问题\n2\n账本里记三种东西\n证据\n一条记录一个陈述，一件事用几句话说仍是一条\n第 1 版 §2；Alex 判 11\n。记逐字引文、能单独读懂的一句话摘要、说话人、原始说话人和原始日期（不知道就写不知道，不填报道日\nD220\n）、来源层。同一条证据可以挂到几个问题上，推力写在挂接上\nPRD 06\n。每一条进来的证据都单独存下；和已有证据的关系（同一说法、修订）由匹配标出，作为意见，两条都留着\nD202、D204\n。原始说话人、原始日期、数字都相同的几条（说话人或日期不知道的不算相同），由代码认作同一条：判断时只给看一次，写明几家报道，每条仍照存\nD248 第 7 条；改 D204 的\"两条都给判断看\"\n。这条在打开之前要先在同一说法的金标上量两件事（金标按这条规则实际会合的组取，对着原文读）：它会不会藏掉不同或更丰富的说法（比如多一句限定），它依赖的原始说话人、原始日期两个字段准不准；量完之前，照 D204 两条都给判断看。\n问题\n问题句、结果档、到期日写下不改；当前判断随证据更新，到期定格\nPRD 06\n。问题分标的和主题，按开题理由分五类（第 3 节）。问题从三处来：文章里的候选问题；用户的备忘录\nPRD 06 State\n：\"A thesis from the user's memo is not yet judged until evidence first links to it\"；分析员开始覆盖一家公司时种下的驱动问题\nD208，设计未建\n。\n研究组\n一件正在进行的事（一个产品换代及其部署、一笔融资或收购、一个政策过程、一轮投资或需求周期），把几个问题组织在一起，常常跨公司。它不是问题，也不代替开题判断。公司自己的报表行（收入、毛利率、费用、税率、资本开支）不带研究组，除非这一行本身就在追这样一件事；它也可以加入别的问题建的组\nD165\n。\n不进账本的：\n归档找不到家的证据，记\nno home\n扔掉\nD209\n。评估时逐条读被扔的，分四种：抽取的垃圾；库里缺这个问题（覆盖缺口）；问题在却没挂上（归档错）；该开没开（开题错）。各归各的环节\n第 1 版 §7\n。存储的表结构归 PRD 06 管。\n3\n问题的五类：账本的几大类型\n开题理由是 D184 定的四类，D236 把主驱动量扩到产品节点，所以写成五类。判两件事，互不顶替：这是谁的问题（标的还是主题）；为什么值得开（开题理由）。\n类\n开题理由\n标的或主题\n问什么\n量还是事件\n期间和到期\n核对材料\nsettled_by\n出处\nA\n主驱动量\nmain_driver\n标的\n承载公司价值的那个年度量，不管公司指不指引：撑起业务的分部收入、毛利率、资本开支；跨行业如银行的净利息收入、零售商的同店销售、公用事业的费率基数、航空公司的燃油成本（当它是争论焦点时）。不是每一行报表数\n量，五档\n公司财年的今年或明年；到期日是这个数的发布日\n公布这个数的公开材料，通常是年报\nD184：\nsettled_by\n是 \"the public material that resolves it\"；PRD 06 梯子第 5 条\nB\n主产品节点\nmain_driver\n标的\n承载公司价值的那一代产品哪天走到能带来收入的那一步（通常是开始出货）；发布、送样是它的证据，只有会分别下注的里程碑（新药的三期数据和获批）各一题\nD248 第 3 条\n事件，五档，或是非两档\nPRD 06 梯子第 1 条\n能判出结果的那天\n公司公告、发货确认\nD236\nC\n约束性义务\nbinding_obligation\n标的\n公司以自己名义承担的：为别人的债务、租约或收入担保或提供信用支持；借钱给别人；承诺购买（向别人买产能或服务也算）；为客户的产能或收入兜底；或写明规模、尚未完成的入股或收购\nD236；收购按 D243 第 7 条\n。规模要大到能改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险才开；够不上的单笔入股、收购、租约、授信不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判\nD248 第 6 条\n。不算：自己借钱、自己的租约、已完成或没写规模的投资。D239 选定的 role-v29 原文：\"Its own borrowing, its own leases and a completed or unsized investment are not obligations\"；D238 的记录写作 \"the company's own borrowing and leases and a completed or unsized stake do not\"\n还没签：是非两档。已经承担（一笔或一个计划）：金额，五档\n检查日最多在文章后 18 个月\nD185\n；已承担的按下一份年报\n签约公告、年报承诺附注\nD184、D185、D210、D236、D238、D243\nD\n共同量\nshared_quantity\nD191、D243 第 2 条\n；现行提示词和代码还叫\nindustry_aggregate\n，按这一页改\n主题\n任何一家公司之外、好几家都会受它影响的量或价格：几家公司的合计（云厂商资本开支）、价格（内存合约价、油价）、利率。同一总量的另一种人群、另一种说法是这个量的证据\n量，五档\n自然年的今年或明年。同一个量、同一年只有一个问题，由代码按\"主题＋量＋期间\"判\nD197\n；量的类别是\nother\n的不算\n几家公司年报、统计发布、官方决定\nD184、D191、D197、D243；PRD 06\nE\n政策阶段\npolicy_stage\n问的是一家公司的收入、利润、份额或产品进度，算标的，即使由政策驱动；问的是世界上的事（许可制度、利率、合计支出），算主题。D184 采纳的 role-v12 原文：\"A question about a company's revenue, profit, share or product progress is an asset thesis; put its ticker in object, even when policy or macroeconomics drives it\"\n一个政策或监管过程到了哪一步，写明谁说了算\n事件，五档或两档\n能判出那一步的那天。文章没写日期的待决监管事项，用本财年末（role-v12 原文：\"A pending regulatory decision may lack a date; use the current fiscal year-end as its due date\"）；这个日期是研究员定的检查日，要标明\n评审 §1 第 1 条\n官方决定、公报\nD184\n例子（跨行业）：\nA：\nNVIDIA FY2027 数据中心收入；一家地区银行 FY2026 净利息收入；一家公用事业公司 2027 年费率基数。\nB：\n下一代机架系统能否在 2027 年底前开始出货；一家车企的新平台能否在 2026 年内开始交付。\nC：\nNVIDIA 为云客户产能兜底的义务在 FY2027 年报达到多少；一家保险公司是否在 2027 年中前为合资公司 5 亿美元债务签署担保。\nD：\n五家最大云厂商 2026 年合计资本开支；2027 年 DRAM 合约价相对 2026 年的变化、美联储 2026 年最后一次会议后的利率上限。\nE：\n中国是否在 2027-01-31 前正式批准本国公司进口 H200；美国是否在某日前对某类芯片实行出口许可。这两题问的是许可这件世界上的事，所以是主题，role-v12 原文的例子就是 \"licensing\"。一个监管决定对一家公司收入或产品进度的影响，问成那家公司的量或产品节点时，是标的问题（同一句原文）。\n五类之外的东西去哪\n七类驱动不是问题类型。\n它们是一条陈述为什么重要的理由，用来判分量和归档。D208 设计的\"每家公司种下的驱动问题\"由分析员开始覆盖时写好，不从文章里开；还没建。\n别家公司自己的量\n（对手的份额、客户的资本开支）：D187 定了\"任何公司自己的量是那家公司的问题，私营公司也算\"，但还没实现，要先在覆盖名单里加私营公司这一类。D155 定了\"公司清单可以作为初始参考，但清单外不能忽略\"。现行代码在匹配时跳过名单外的对象\nPRD 06 Step 4\n。所以抽取照常可以提这样的问题，它要过第 4 节的测试；名单外的现在会被匹配跳过，这不算抽取错。\n宏观量\n（利率、通胀）：按 D 类共同量开题\nD243 第 2 条\n。只来自一篇纯宏观文章时，只有主题在名单上才开\nD191 第 3 条\n。宏观对每家公司的正负，在判断那家公司时读主题块决定\nD191 第 1 条、D195\n。\n开题理由是封闭的\nD184\n：一件事属不属于五类之一，按上表逐条看，比如一个监管决定看它是不是 E 类。哪一类都不属于的不开；它是不是证据、挂到哪，照第 5 节和归档规则。\n问题怎么写：PRD 06 的梯子规则，不重写\n一个问题只问一个量的一个期间，或一件事；能各自朝相反方向走、而且分析员会分别下注的部分要分开\nPRD 06 梯子第 3 条；\"分别下注\"是 D248 加的，PRD 06 签字后更新\n，分开的每一题都要过第 4 节的测试。\n写成问句，不写成断言\nPRD 06 Rationale\n。\n结果档可核对、不重叠、不留缝，到期恰好一档成立；中间一档写明和什么比；\"小幅\"\"部分\"这种不能核对的说法不用，部分状态写成单独一档\nPRD 06 梯子第 2 条\n。标的问题的档描述业务结果；主题问题的档只写结局，不写好坏\nPRD 06、D186\n。\n到期日：量是它的发布日；事件是能判出结果的那天。公司的量用公司财年，别的用自然年，只开今年和明年\nPRD 06 梯子第 5 条；约束性义务例外，D185\n。\n4\n开题标准\n一个问题值得单独追，要同时满足三件事：它的答案可能改变仓位；能在一个日期用公开材料判出落在哪一档；库里没有问题在问同一件事。\n开多少个是判断的结果，不是目标\n第 1 版 §6；评审 §1\n。\n颗粒度按一个高端对冲基金分析员：只有会单独下注的大事开题，鸡毛蒜皮不开题（它的陈述留不留照第 5 节判）；同一个赌注的几种说法是一题，截止日、里程碑、财年和日历年各按本节\"同一题、续期，还是另一题\"里的那一条判\nD248\n。\n另有一条前提：新问题要有独立的投资判断；一个新产品、一个新数字、一个更窄的范围，单凭这些不开\nD201\n。\n四个测试\n第 1 版 §6\n值得追\n写得出\"这个结果会改变哪项收入、利润、现金或信用风险、竞争位置\"吗？写不出就留作证据，不开题。\"数字很大\"不算理由。单笔交易、产品、项目，规模够不上改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险的，不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判\nD248 第 6 条：问的就是小交易、小产品\n；这一测试的其余理由（包括竞争位置）不变。\n独立意义\n能写出两个情景吗：其他问题的答案不变，只有这一题相反，而对业务的判断确实不同？写不出就优先作为证据\n评审 §1 第 3、8 条\n。评审里的一个判例：NVIDIA FY2027 总收入题判为数据中心收入题的证据，理由是\"该段明确说主要增长来自数据中心，未提出独立的其他业务争论\"。这是对那一段材料的判断，别的材料要逐个判。\n能核对\n有一个日期和一份公开材料能把答案放进事先固定的档吗？没有就重写问题。到期没有材料记\"未能判定\"，不算错。\n不是重复\n和已有问题比，只是预测值、措辞、来源、方向或同一总量的另一种口径不同，最终核对的是同一个结果吗？是就是同一题。抽取看不到库\nD211\n，这一条在抽取时只对同一篇文章里提出的几个问题判；和库里的比由匹配做。\n另外三条写法检查\n评审 §1 第 1、5、6 条\n。没过的问题不能照原样开，要重写；重写不了就不开，材料作证据。\n原文支持：\n主体、量或事件、时间依据都能在原文找到。原文没给日期、而规则允许研究员定检查日的，用那个日期并标明：没写日期的待决监管事项用本财年末（第 3 节 E 类）；还没签的约束性义务用文章后 18 个月以内能核对签约的那天\nD185、D210\n。不把\"拟议\"\"预计\"写成\"已签\"\"已完成\"。\n口径完整：\n算谁、不算谁、算什么写清楚；支出、融资、担保、订单、收入不混。\n时间正确：\n原文支持这个期间，不把几年的平均写成今年。\n不开的\n第 1 版 §6 的清单\n不开\n出处\n它变成什么\n季度数字和季度指引\n第 1 版 §6；PRD 06 梯子第 6 条\n证据\n同一段指引里的每一行（\"费用率、税率是模型输入，不是争论\"）\n第 1 版 §6\n证据\n没日期的\"护城河\"\"战略\"\n第 1 版 §6\n证据\n高管任免、股东投票、回购授权；章程\n第 1 版 §6；章程出自 D239 选定的 role-v29 原文（\"executive appointments, shareholder votes or bylaws\"）\n证据或不留\n例行诉讼程序\n第 1 版 §6\n证据\n客户的部署计划（\"它是供应商需求和产品问题的证据，除非改变供应商自己的现金或信用风险\"）\n第 1 版 §6\n证据；改变公司自己的现金或信用风险时按 C 类判\n以约束性义务为理由开：公司自己借钱、自己的租约、已完成或没写规模的投资。它们不是约束性义务；对别的问题仍可以是证据，或按别的开题理由判\nD236、D238\n证据\n把远期年度预测改装成事件\n第 1 版 §6\n证据\n后年及以后的年度量（约束性义务除外）\nPRD 06 梯子第 5 条；D185\n证据\n同一个量的另一个估计、另一种口径、另一个来源\n第 1 版 §6 测试 4\n那个已开问题的证据，由匹配并入\n同一题、续期，还是另一题：匹配这一步的事，抽取不判库里的\n总规则：\n同一个结果能判掉两题，就是同一题；答案能分开走、而且分析员会分别下注的，才是另一题\nD248\n。\n同一题：\n同一个结果能同时判两个问题。另一个估计、来源、措辞、方向\n第 1 版 §6 测试 4\n；同一总量的另一种人群或代用口径（D190 碰撞裁决提示 collision-v1：\"another population or proxy of one aggregate\"；D197 引的 role-v12 原文：\"another population or another wording of the same aggregate is evidence to the shown aggregate thesis\"）。候选并进已开问题，用它的结果档和到期日\nPRD 06\n。只有以共同量理由开题、带量的候选由代码自动判，其余是匹配和碰撞裁决的判断。\n同一个计划的几种说法\n（担保、融资承诺、信用支持……），最后核对的是同一个数，是一题\nD248 第 1 条\n。\n同一件事的不同截止日：\n早的那题开着时，晚的截止日是它的证据，不另开；早的到期没有结果、事情还在追，系统开下一个截止日的续期题，带上并进来的证据，不等新文章\nD248 第 2 条\n。\n同一代产品的几个里程碑：\n一题，问能带来收入的那一步（通常是开始出货）；送样、发布作证据；会分别下注的里程碑（新药的三期数据和获批）各一题\nD248 第 3 条\n。\n公司自己的量：\n只按财年开题；按日历年的估计是财年那题的证据，两种年份差得多时分量低一点\nD248 第 4 条；PRD 06 梯子第 5 条\n。\n身份：\n事件和政策题里模型写的期间起点，不拿来挡合并；对象、事件、截止日照旧核对\nD248 第 5 条\n。\n续期：\n同一个量或同一串事件的下一个期间\nPRD 06 Succession、D190\n。\n另一题：\n同一件事里答案能分开走、分析员会分别下注的：有自己驱动的组成部分；同一笔交易或同一份附注里两个各自会变、分别盯着的数；另一件事\nD190 碰撞裁决的四种；D248 第 1 条收窄了\"担保和融资\"\n。\n约束性义务：\n\"一笔或一个计划\"一题\nD210\n；一笔交易是不是某个已按金额开题的计划的一部分，由匹配判，是就并入\nD248 第 1 条\n。还没签的单笔交易是\"签不签\"的是非题，照旧可以开，不并进计划的金额题\nD210；PRD 06；D248 未改\n。\n共同量：\n以共同量理由开题、带量的候选（量的类别是\nother\n的不算），由代码按\"主题＋量＋期间\"判同一题；政策阶段和别的事件保留按事件判的身份；不带这个开题理由的主题量由模型判\nD197\n。\n谁判，谁查\n内容\n谁\n测试 1–3、同一篇文章里的测试 4、属于哪一类、问题怎么写\n抽取的模型（判断）\n结果档形状、期间是真日期、到期在文章之后、季度数、年限、约束性义务 18 个月、\nsettled_by\n不为空\n代码：只查形状，不判重要性\nD154、D155、D184\n和库里的同一题、续期、另一题\n匹配（Jev）和碰撞裁决\nD190\n；共同量由代码\nD197\n年限按公司财年数：2026 年 7 月时 NVIDIA 在 FY2027（2027 年 1 月结束），所以 FY2028 是明年。代码原来按自然年算，把 FY2028 当成后年拒掉；在 v29 答案的 40 个带年份的候选里拒错了 2 个。已修，测试是\ntests/test_fiscal_year_horizon.py\nD243\n。\n5\n证据标准\n5.1 留的规则和四类\n一个在追这家公司的分析员，读到会在本周笔记里写下来，因为它让某个问题的答案更可能或更不可能。\n问题不限于库里已有的，也不限于本公司：客户、供应商、竞争者、监管、宏观都算\n第 1 版 §2；D208 七类驱动\n。\n整体倾向谨慎，对增量敏感。\n杂音很大，不是什么都要抽，不是什么都算线索。判一句话之前先问：这是新信息吗？它让哪个问题的答案变了？一件新发生的事、一个新数字、一个新的具体判断，是增量，不能漏；重复常识、描述长期状况、泛泛的看法，是杂音\nD243\n。增量和杂音的具体界线按 5.3 各行和边界一起判\nD250\n。\n从第一段读到最后一段，分析员会记的（按本节和 5.3 各行边界一起判）都留。一页有八条就是八条，不挑\nD199\n。它们通常是下面四类之一；四类描述的是会记的陈述长什么样，不是\"属于四类就一定留\"，留不留由上面的规则判；具体不留的行和边界见 5.3。\n公司自己的数字、指引或决定\n第 1 版 §2\n\"同店销售本季增长 4.2%\"；\"管理层把全年收入指引定在 120–130 亿美元\"；\"董事会批准 100 亿美元回购\"；\"公司取消软件订阅费，生产使用免费\"\nAlex 判 8，Omniverse\n。\n客户、供应商、竞争者、伙伴的有规模、有日期或有名字的动作，以及它们公布的数字\n第 1 版 §2：三者有一个就够\n第 1 版的例子\"Google Cloud 季度收入 248 亿美元，增长 82%，积压超过 5000 亿美元\"\nAlex 判 11\n；\"最大客户说明年订单砍五分之一\"；\"主要供应商说 2027 年产能翻倍\"；\"对手在欧洲降价 15%\"；\"一家银行选用竞争对手的系统\"；\"一家软件公司把本公司的模型集成进政府系统\"。后两条有名字、没金额，也留\nD240\n。\n具名一方的主张或估计\n第 1 版 §2\n\"CFO 说十年前建的工厂仍满负荷\"；\"分析师把明年 EPS 预期上调到 4.10 美元\"；\"做空机构指控公司虚增应收 20 亿美元\"；\"内存厂说 2027 年短缺最严重，持续到 2030\"。\n政策或监管的决定或步骤\n第 1 版 §2\n\"监管机构批准该药第二个适应症\"；\"政府要求该产品出口须申请许可\"；\"25 家公司联名反对过早限制开放权重模型\"；\"财长说将调查某国公司是否窃取知识产权\"。\n典型的主张和背景\nD243 第 5 条\n：Alex 的原话是\"这种典型的主张背景属于常识，基本上抽出来也没有太多的意义除非带数字和具体的叙事不然都是属于不采纳\"。常识，就是跟这家公司的分析员读这篇之前就已经知道的：谁领先、谁在竞争、需求强、供给紧、融资难这类说法，和对已知状况的重述。这类陈述没有数字和具体的叙事就不收。不是常识的，是新信息，比如一份新报告的判断、一个新的市场数据、刚发生的事的细节，照上面的规则判，分析员会记就留。\n常识，不收：\n\"博通 CEO 说能从英伟达抢份额\"；\"Blackwell 和 Rubin 仍然供不应求\"；\"融资会是扩大算力最大的障碍\"；\"内存厂避开了以往的产能过剩\"；\"博通预计明年随着新客户推出定制芯片大幅增长\"。\n新信息，收：\n一位科技公司高管对媒体说\"中国国产 AI 芯片厂商已经全部卖光，客户在考虑性能更低的芯片\"；\"旧型号 GPU 的租金在涨\"；研究机构说\"这让 Rubin Ultra 暂时没有成熟的扩展方案，给对手留出了空间\"；\"华为现在占中国 AI 芯片市场约一半，这个市场约 500 亿美元\"。\n有数字也要过上面的规则：\n作者说\"英特尔多年领先，占 CPU 市场 59%\"，和英伟达的问题关系不大，分析员不会记。\n5.2 不完整也是证据\n预测、传闻、反证、远期、条件情景都是证据；它们的层级和确定性另记。不因为\"还没发生\"\"不是官方\"\"离现在远\"\"和我们方向相反\"就不留；一个真实的反例可能比十个顺风数字更重要\n第 1 版 §2\n。有名有姓的动作缺金额、缺日期也是证据，分量可以低一点\nD240\n。不留清单只按 5.3 各行和边界删，不能用来删掉标准要留的陈述：\"a reject rule never removes what the standard keeps\"\nD240\n。\n5.3 不留的\n第 1 版 §2 的清单，加 D238、D243 的行\n\"按同一行补的例子\"一列是我按同一行举的例子。\n不留\n第 1 版的例子\n按同一行补的例子\n边界\n没有业务规模的产品、游戏、驱动、跑分细节\n\"这张卡 415W\"；\"Geekbench 跑分\"\n接口、功率、旧型号的支持周期\n对手拿它和本公司产品比的说法不能用这一行删掉，按 5.1 判（Alex 判 4：AMD 2.4 倍是辅助）；伙伴、客户采用或集成的动作不是产品细节（5.1 第 2 类）；公司自己、伙伴或第三方发布的对比（\"比闭源模型便宜 10 倍\"、\"同样功率多放 40% GPU\"、第三方测试）是产品细节，不收；同一句里伙伴、客户采用或集成的动作不因这一条删，照旧按 5.1 判\nD250 第 3 条\n股价涨跌、投资人仓位、技术面\n\"本月跌 13%\"；\"Burry 做空\"\n期权成交、基金买卖\n股价反应背后的那条新闻不能用这一行删掉，按 5.1 判；\"财报后股价反应\"可选\n第 1 版 §10.2\n；在研公司的估值倍数、市值、孤零零的目标价可选\nD243 第 4 条\n；别的股价涨跌照旧不留\n作者的一般性风险清单和没有推导的看法\n\"如果 AI 退潮会受伤\"\n\"这只股票该持有\"；\"芯片指数是纯粹的高估\"\n有假设、有推导、有时点的条件估值要留\n第 1 版 §10.1\n；具名分析师对一件已记下的事的解读（\"推迟会给对手留出窗口\"），没有新事实或推算的，不收，有新事实或推算的按 5.1 判\nD250 第 4 条\n记者的背景和框架\n\"A100 2020 年上市\"；\"海湾国家花了几十亿但仍依赖它\"\n\"公司成立于 1998 年\"；通用的技术讲解\n没有数字和具体叙事的主张和背景不收；怎么分见 5.1\nD243 第 5 条\n；只描述一个现成安排（合作、客户在用、已有部署）、没有新动作的，是背景，不收，文中没写日期也一样\nD250 第 1 条\n没有数字也没有决定的政治、战略表态\n\"分散供应商不能减少政治依赖\"\n大使馆说\"立场一贯\"\n官员宣布的政策步骤不能用这一行删掉，按 5.1 判（第 4 类）\n同一事实的复述\n一篇稿子用五句话说一个部署是一条；三家媒体转同一个独家，原始出处写同一个\n标题和正文说同一件事\n已知事实的重述\n两个月后再说一遍 Q1 收入、指引、已公布的回购\n抽取看不到库，只能从文字和日期看。重提的过去实际数可选；重提的季度指引和仍然有效的预测、计划按本来分量；看不出是旧事的不算重提\nD243 第 6 条\n；只描述现成安排、没有新动作的，按背景判（见\"记者的背景和框架\"一行），其余仍按这一行判\nD250 第 1 条\n模板\n署名、作者简介、经纪商页脚、前瞻性声明、广告\n页面上和本文无关的内容（相关文章预告、推荐列表、别的新闻标题）\n只有形容的引语\n\"a pivotal moment\"；\"heterogeneous inference\"（role-v18 原文的例子）\nD243 第 10 条\n新加坡转运案件的概况和被告个人资产\n可选，收不收都不扣分\nD231\n另外一条来自后来的决定：\n和公司所有市场都无关的别家公司的估值、股价、交易\n不留（D238：\"another company's valuation, share price or deals that touch none of the company's markets\"）。别家公司的动作和经营数字不能用这一行删掉，它们留不留按 5.1 的规则判：要说得出它通过需求、供给、竞争、定价这类路径动了在研公司的哪个问题；只因同一篇文章、同一场活动或同一个生态挨着、说不出这条路径的，不收\nD250 第 2 条\n。\n不在清单上的句子，用 5.1 的规则逐句判（分析员会不会记）；金标里把这类判断标出来供复核。\n5.4 一条记录必须保住的意思\n第 1 版 §3，原样\n六格里只填这一条用得上的\nD220\n。\n格\n要保住\n丢了就错的例子\n谁\n原始判断者；是本人估计还是别人实测\nAMD 自己的模拟说成独立实测\n什么\n业务、指标或动作\n\"收入增长 82%\"丢了\"收入\"\n多少\n金额、比例、数量\n四家 7450 亿写成五家\n哪个期间\n预测或事实覆盖的期间；\"今年\"按公司财年或自然年写都算对\nD233\n，余下时间不改成全年\n\"今年余下时间\"改成 FY2027 全年\n什么条件\n推导的假设；计划、上限、\"最多\"\n\"最多 4 万颗\"写成已部署 4 万颗\n预测还是已发生\n指引、预计、商谈、传闻不等于已实现、已签约\n\"商谈 6000 亿\"写成\"已承诺\"\n原始发言日不知道就写不知道，不能填报道日\nD220\n。一条记录单独读要能懂，条件不能只放在旁边另一条里\n第 1 版 §3，引主分支 D251；两个分支的决定编号各自独立，D227\n。不必要的：转述机构、普通发言场合、付费方案全名；漏了不扣原意分，\n编造了仍算记录错误\n第 1 版 §3\n。没有来源证实的结论，可以作为分析员的推断写出，但不能写成来源说的事实\nD218\n。\n边界\n第 1 版 §2\n：Sharon 合同是供需的辅助证据，不是担保金额的证据；NVL72x2 取消归产品进度\nAlex 判 10\n。\n5.5 来源层\nPRD 06 的四层；D238 采纳的 role-v29 原文\nPRD 06 的定义：事实＝正式动作、公布的数字、完成的交易；当事人陈述＝公司指引、管理层表态、官员讲话；第三方观点＝卖方分析师、媒体分析、专家；传闻＝匿名来源的说法和没有出处的说法。层级跟着\"是谁确认的\"走，没有固定分数；二十家转载一个独家是一个来源。\nrole-v29 提示词的原文（D238；Alex 在 D239 选它作为抽取请求；D245 换成的 role-v42、D246 换成的 role-v51、D247 换成的 role-v53 这一句都未改）：\"fact for official actions, published actual figures or completed transactions; party_statement for company guidance, management statements or officials' remarks; third_party_view for analyst, media or expert opinions; rumour for claims the item credits to unnamed sources or calls unconfirmed; an announcement or completed deal the item reports is a fact even when the reporter names no source.\"\n这两处列了四层，下表把它们排成一张。一句话同时符合两行时，取表里上面那行\nD243 第 9 条\n。\n说的是什么\n层\n1. 文章说来自没具名的人，或文章自己说未经证实（即使说的是一项公告或一个数）\n传闻\n2. 正式动作、公布的实际数字、完成的交易；文章报道的公告或已完成的交易（即使记者没写出处）\n事实\n3. 公司的指引、管理层的表态、官员的讲话\n当事人陈述\n4. 分析师、媒体、专家的看法和估计\n第三方观点\n5. 以上都不是、又没有出处的说法（PRD 06：\"unattributed statements\"）\n传闻\n一条记录里既有已发生的部分、又有计划或传闻的细节，按主要说法定层，次要部分的性质写进摘要；不拆成两条，除非是两件事\nD243 第 9 条\n。层级之间的关系：后来的确认和先前的传闻都留在账本里；匹配判断两条的关系（同一说法、修订或新的），修订的条件过了才记修订，没过就作为新的一条存下\nD202、D204\n；同一说法在判断时怎么显示，见第 2 节\nD248 第 7 条\n；判断时只有事实能确定到期落在哪一档\nPRD 06\n。\n6\n分量\n分量问\"这条对判断多重要\"，分四级。按投资后果判它碰到的是什么；核心和辅助的界线按信息量定\n第 1 版 §4；D243 第 1 条\n。缺金额、缺日期只影响分到哪一档，不是不留的理由\nD240\n。分量不由数字多不多、是不是官方、库里有没有页、和我们方向是否相同、文章篇幅决定\n第 1 版 §4\n；离现在远不是不留的理由\nD216\n，它落在核心还是辅助由判断定。\n等级\n什么样\n例子\n核心\n碰到关键判断，并且带着能定位答案的信息。关键判断：收入、利润、融资或信用风险、需求、关键产品进度、关键投入的供给和价格；相对在研公司判，包括它的约束性义务和它承载价值的那一代产品及其配置、过渡方案。能定位答案的信息：一个量（金额、比例、数量；在谈的金额也算，\"在谈\"写进意思里）、一个确定状态，或一个带期间的预测。对公司不是小事，也不是别家公司自己的经营数字\n分析师预计今年余下收入增长 82%、2027 年 41%；云巨头 2026 年合计支出 6500 亿美元；内存厂说短缺持续到 2030；为客户 1050 亿美元租约担保；公司宣布为云客户产能兜底的新计划（确定状态，没给金额）；银行指引全年净利息收入降 3–5%；监管机构批准主力药或定下裁决日；主要供应商宣布 9 月起涨价 8%；交易对手披露公司 34 亿美元的采购承诺；据报道在谈最多 6000 亿美元的支持\n辅助\n支持或反驳上面的判断，或给出可检验的估值假设；碰到关键判断但没有能定位答案的信息；别家公司自己的经营数字（收入、利润率、积压订单）；对公司规模来说是小事的；离现在越远、判断下来只是辅助的预测\n对手自己模拟的 2.4 倍性能比较；公司取消软件订阅费；三条开放模型政策表态；Sharon 六年 72 MW 合同；FY2028 EPS 一致预期；12.76 × 25 的条件估值；一家银行选用对手系统（没写金额）；伙伴把公司模型集成进政府系统（没写金额）；内存厂公布的季度收入和利润率；客户公布的订单积压；客户园区\"规划最多 17 万颗 GPU\"\n可选\n帮助理解市场预期，省略不损害论证；留不留都不扣分\n财报后股价反应；在研公司的估值倍数、市值、孤零零的目标价；生态数字；重提的过去实际数；新加坡案件\nD231、D243 第 3、4、6 条\n不收\n第 5.3 节的各行；与判断无关\n模板；产品参数；个人仓位；空泛风险；没有数字和具体叙事的主张\n判法\n先判留不留\n按 5.1 判分析员会不会记，不会就不收。在 5.3 的不留清单上的，先看那一行的\"边界\"一列：写着\"不能用这一行删掉\"或\"按 5.1 判\"的（对手比较、伙伴或客户的采用或集成、股价反应背后的新闻、官员宣布的政策步骤、带来新事实或推算的解读）按 5.1 判，留下的按下面两步定分量；写着\"要留\"或\"按本来分量\"的（条件估值、重提的指引和仍然有效的预测、计划）照办；写着可选的归可选；其余不收。\n是可选的那几样吗？\n在研公司的估值倍数、市值、孤零零的目标价，财报后股价反应，生态数字，重提的过去实际数，新加坡案件，归可选。带推导的条件估值例外，是辅助\n第 1 版 §10.1\n。\n核心还是辅助\n碰到关键判断、带着能定位答案的信息、对公司不是小事、不是别家公司自己的经营数字，四条都满足是核心；其余是辅助。别家公司直接说出关键投入或需求的状况（短缺到哪年、明年花多少），按关键判断算。预测离现在越远，越可能只是辅助，这由判断定：FY2028 EPS 一致预期是辅助，Alex 说\"太久远了\"；内存厂说短缺 2027 年最严重、持续到 2030 是核心，它覆盖今明两年。\n\"确定状态\"\n：已经签了、宣布了、开始了、批准了、取消了、推迟到某个日期了、定下了裁决日；没有量的\"在谈\"\"在考虑\"\"可能\"\"在探索\"\"打算\"不算。确定状态以陈述当天为时间，不要求生效日。\n\"带期间的预测\"\n：分析师预计今年余下收入增长 82%；内存厂说短缺持续到 2030 年。确定不确定看说法本身的内容；靠不靠得住另记在来源层。\nAlex 拍过的分量\nD215；gold-check.md，D229\n：收入增速核心；内存短缺核心；AMD 跑分辅助；三条政策辅助；云厂商支出核心；Omniverse 辅助；Sharon \"辅助属于供给需求\"；商谈最多 6000 亿支持 OpenAI 核心（在谈的金额）；投资 SB Energy 15 亿核心；Broadcom AI 半导体收入 +143%、Google Cloud 三个数辅助（别家公司的经营数字）；FY2028 EPS 辅助（远期）；逐案审核、有拒绝辅助（没有量和确定结果）。\n分量只在评估时判。\n分量只在金标和测量里判，不进账本，不进抽取的输出\nD240\n；账本只存来源层。抽取的模型决定留不留、怎么写，以及提哪些候选问题\n第 1 版 §1\n。\n仿真里剩下的分歧几乎都在核心和辅助这条线上：\"对公司规模来说是不是小事\"从一句话里常常看不出来（第 12 节，第三轮）。所以核心和辅助分开报，评估时允许读者间合理的不同判\nD226、D228\n。\n7\n研究观点标准\n判断这一步归 PRD 06、D198、D201 管；这里只列和抽取共用的。\n观点是什么：\n一个关于业务和世界的、有论证的看法；立场来自论证和市场已经定价的东西相比；报表行是它的证据，不是它的定义\nAGENTS §8\n。\n判断读什么：\n一个问题的判断看到的，是它仍然有效的证据里的两部分：新到的（在它倒数第五次判断之后挂上的；判断不满五次时全部显示）和依据（最近十次判断里被引用过的）。其余仍在账本里有效，请求里写明还有多少条没显示；答案只能引用和推动显示了的\nD198；从 judging-v5 起，v4 的配置照旧读全部有效证据\n。依赖问题的结论和理由、标的问题的主题块也读\nPRD 06、D195\n。主题结论变了，同一轮里排队重判符合条件的标的问题；主题的结论不作为证据引用\nD195\n。\n研究的交付物是论证：\n保留不可改的来源记录，和一份有版本的研究论证，写明支持和反对的来源、是否独立、变化和局限\nD201\n。\n研究用途：\n评估判断时，读者按四级看它引用的每条证据对那个具体结论撑得住多强：直接可用、有条件可用、线索、不可用；同一条证据对不同结论可以不同\nD221\n。这是评估的读法，不是判断这一步要输出或存下的字段（D221：\"not a numerical confidence score or a new storage layer\"）。用途和分量不同：分量问\"这条多重要\"，用途问\"拿它证明这个结论够不够\"。\n事实和推断分开：\n来源没说的结论可以作为分析员推断写出，但要标明\nD218\n。只有事实能确定落在哪一档\nPRD 06\n。\n重复不是新证据，模型意见一致不是互相印证\nAGENTS §8\n。\n8\n金标：改提示词的测试标准\n下面这份第 3 版金标是历史记录。第 4 版（D248）改了开题的规则，没有改动证据条目（逐条核对见\nresearch/2026-10-30-noise/gold-v4-evidence-check.md\n）；第 5 版（D250）改了证据的四条界线。金标的证据部分按第 5 版重推，两位互不知情的读者逐条判，分歧对着原文定，另存成新文件冻结，每条改动写改前、改后和理由，第 3 版文件不动（D243 第 11 条）。开题部分要按第 4 版逐条重判，重判之前打分程序不报开题的分；证据部分版本对不上，打分程序拒绝打分（D244、D250）。\n第 5 版金标（2026-10-31 冻结）：\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/golden-v5/golden-v5-dev.json\n（sha256\n6f9476bb6a81ea590af273a71565e7c3c0f49908d26274b8482d52cfdee31a21\n）和\ngolden-v5-test.json\n（sha256\naf2145cdc60c34ec1ded0bebcb809800e187d616992ea325919c3ad771da61e3\n）。重判了 537 条（全部要收、可收的证据，引 D238、别家公司、5.1 判断或新加坡案件的不收句，和盲读认为该留的 35 句）：Codex 和 Claude 两位读者各自盲读，96 条进裁定，62 处改动逐条写改前、改后、两位读者的判法和理由（\ngolden-v5/corrections-v5.json\n）。开发集：核心 86（不变）、辅助 151（原 181）、可选 72；不收 770、可选 31。测试集：核心 9、辅助 37（原 42）、可选 1。两篇以中东冲突主题为对象的文章，读者被告知按在研公司读，是说明写错，那几条不改。下面的数字是第 3 版的历史记录。\n按这一页从原文逐篇写的参照，用来量提示词改动。Alex 的要求是\"以上有你自己trace 过的golden set 来做为改prompt测试标准\"\nD241\n。定稿文件：\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/golden-v3/golden-v3-signed.json\n，sha256\n78844bc329fc85000e18ddcab9ea59feb57abe6407cf11bbead4ba8fe4c3c0d3\nD243\n。\n118\n篇测试文章：那一周 98 篇，留出 20 篇\n348\n条要收或可以收的证据，一件事一条\n738\n句不收，各写理由；另有 24 句可选\n1110\n段原文片段，代码核过都在原文里\n28 · 42\n个必须开、可以开的问题；240 个不该开\n每一条记什么\n证据：\n原文逐字片段；属于 5.1 哪一类；定稿分量\nweight\n（核心、辅助、可选）；来源层；谁；必须保住的意思（5.4 用得上的格）；判它的那一句标准；签字前的两套分量和它依赖哪几条，留作记录。\n不收的句子：\n属于 5.3 哪一行，或按 5.1 的理由；\nweight\n是不收或可选。可选的留不留都不扣分。\n问题：\n必须开的（类、开题理由、标的或主题、要点、\nsettled_by\n）；可以开的（另一种合理写法）；不该开的（第 4 节哪一行或哪个测试）。\n没判清的地方：\n两种读法都写出来。\n分量的分布（定稿）\n那一组\n核心\n辅助\n可选\n要收或可收\n不收\n原列不收、现为可选\n那一周 98 篇\n80\n152\n63\n295\n621\n22\n留出 20 篇\n6\n29\n18\n53\n117\n2\n参考答案第 2 版那一周是核心 47、辅助 69、可选 48。辅助多出来的，主要是有名有姓、没写规模的动作（伙伴集成、客户采用、合作）：第 2 版标成\"什么都不留\"，现在按 D240 收为辅助。\n怎么写成、怎么核的\n六个起草者分批写\nClaude opus 各写 20 篇左右，只读标准和原文；参考答案第 2 版只当起点，每条重判。\n合并时统一写法\nmerge.py\n统一各批的写法，每处写理由（\nmerge-log.txt\n）：旧事重提 49 条逐条判；估值倍数和别家公司股价按现行规则处理；中国是否批准 H200 进口改为主题问题。\n逐条读改动\nClaude 读了每批相对参考答案第 2 版新增、删掉、改档的条目：新增约 124 条，改档约 37 条；旧条目没对上的 10 条逐条查过，都在，只是引文片段不同或按旧事重提处理。\n两轮独立盲读\nCodex 读者只读这份标准和原文，不看金标。读者多留的逐条对原文判：金标漏了的补上，其余写明不补的理由。补进来的 49 条，\"谁\"和\"意思\"对着原文补齐（\nadditions-fields.json\n）。\n按签字定稿\nfinalize.py\n按 D243 给每一条定分量，39 处改动逐条写理由（\nfinalize-log.txt\n）：32 条移出要收的一栏，其中 24 条是常识性的主张和背景（第 5 条），1 条是只有形容的引语（第 10 条），7 条和起草者写了理由的不收句是同一句；另有 7 句不收句和要收的条目重复，删掉。这 19 处\"同一句话既留又不留\"，是盲读后补条目时没和不收的句子对照造成的。此外，不收的句子里 24 句按第 3、4、6 条改为可选。\n盲读\n金标条目\n读者也留了\n分量相同\n读者多留\n补进金标\n第一轮：随机 15 篇，补之前\n34\n33\n26\n19\n8\n第一轮：补之后\n42\n41\n32\n11\n第二轮：其余 103 篇，补之前\n229\n194\n162\n144\n40\n第二轮：补之后\n262\n227\n193\n结论：起草稿大约漏了两成，集中在有名有姓、没写规模的采用和合作，和一些带期间的判断。补完以后，独立读者能找到的金标条目约九成。读者多留的 163 条里，48 条是金标漏了、已补；其余 115 条按标准不补：同一件事金标已有、新加坡案件、产品细节、泛泛看法、旧事重提、合作方自己的事、和公司市场无关、只描述长期状况。剩下的不一致集中在核心和辅助的界线（第 1 条）和\"背景还是主张\"（第 5 条），两条都已由 Alex 拍定。记录在\ngolden-v3/spotcheck.md\n、\npass2-decisions.json\n。\n用它之前要知道的\n它是模型写的。\nClaude 起草和裁定、Codex 盲查；按 AGENTS §7 这样标明。Alex 抽查之前，它不是 Alex 的判断。\n留出集要换。\n之前的提示词循环发过这 20 篇，答案读过一部分。按第 1 版 §11，\"看过模型答案的文章只做开发集\"，所以第一次改提示词之前，要另取一批没人看过答案的文章，从原文写参照，同样盲查、定稿、记 sha256。\n发现错了怎么办。\n不改这个文件；另记一份更正，每条写理由和改前改后，测量用更正后的版本并注明\nD243 第 11 条\n。\n按金标打分的程序还没写。\n代码只核结构和计数；意思由没写这次改动的读者判（第 9.2 节）。\n9\n改提示词、测量、迭代\n签字以后的第一件事：把现行提示词（role-v29）和这一页逐句对一遍，列出不一致的地方，再按 9.3 一类一类改。一般的规则全文在\nAGENTS.md\n§6（写提示词）和 §7（测量和迭代），这里只写到这一步怎么用，出处标在后面。\n9.1 改提示词\n每一句都对得上这一页。\n提示词里每一句关于留、不留、分量、开题的话，在这一页找得到出处；找不到的，是给 Alex 的提议，不是改动\nAGENTS §6\n。\n写\"不留\"之前先核它删不掉要留的。\n分析员会记的陈述，缺金额、缺日期本身不是不留的理由；分量另判，可能低一点\nD240\n。Alex 的原话：\"我不是和你说过不要这么死板吗？……你应该把它记录下来，只是可能档位低一点\"。\n通用的对冲基金大脑。\n每一句、每个例子对银行、药企、零售、芯片读起来一样；例子跨行业，不点名覆盖的公司，不取自测试文章\nD237；AGENTS §6\n。\n不靠加长。\n读整个请求，就像模型读到的那样（系统提示、schema、内容）；列出矛盾、为别的情况写的规则、逼出缺陷的说法；删、并、拆。缺一句才加，别处删掉同样多；报改前改后的字数和 token\nAGENTS §6\n。\n分类写成封闭集合。\n每类两三个跨行业的例子，加走法（从第一段读到最后一段）和停止点（\"一页有八条就是八条\"）\nAGENTS §6\n。例子就是指令：和 schema、读答案的代码不一致的例子删掉。\n发之前先免费核一遍。\n写一份照提示词作答的假答案，过一遍全部代码检查；契约句逐条对金标该收和该拒的例子读一遍；确认发出去的字节和生产一样；\nscripts/preflight.py\n把存下的答案重放一遍\nAGENTS §5、§6\n。\n9.2 测量\n一把尺子。\n这一页 → 定稿金标 → 下面的达标线。另一把尺子在用，比如旧参考答案、评审打分表、提示词自己的类别，就是缺陷，先修掉再测\nD241；AGENTS §7\n。\n计数单位。\n一条\"不收\"的金标条目，只有带着它的那条记录本身不是金标判为核心、辅助或可选的同一件事时，才算留下；引文里顺带带出的不收半句，是那件事的同一条记录，不算误收。是不是同一件事，代码的计数辅助先算，有出入的由读者对着原文判\nD250 第 5 条；第 2 节\"一件事用几句话说仍是一条\"\n。\n不在还在改的尺子上给自己打分。\n金标先给 Alex 看、冻结，再开始改提示词；意思由没写这次改动的读者按金标判；代码只核结构和计数\nAGENTS §7\n。\n被扔的逐条读。\n按四种归因计数（第 2 节），从最大的一类修起\nD209\n。\n总数只看三次发送。\n一次发送只能说明机制；直接读（每篇发一次，每个答案和原文并排读，D238）用来找机制，它的数是一次发送的数，要这样标明。回归先报，每个数都报\nAGENTS §7\n。\n达标线是第 1 版 §11，逐字照抄在下面的引线内：\n单位\n：一篇原文里的一个研究事项（可由多条证据表达）。参照在任何发送之前写好并冻结（sha256），从原文写，不看模型答案；看过模型答案的文章只做开发集，留出集另取没看过的文章。\n每个参照项记四件事\n（gold v5 §二）：选了没有（kept / omitted，拆几条合几条只算一次）；原意（correct / partial / wrong / absent）；可核查（引文和上下文够不够）；记录问题（原始日期、期间、归属、确定性、错来源、重复、推断未标明，各标 material / record_only）。\n分开报，不合成总分\n：核心 N 项里 correct / partial / wrong / absent 各多少；辅助同样；误收率 = 留下的\"不收\"项 ÷ 评过的\"不收\"项；多留的垃圾按条数（我读，代码核结构）；每个 material 问题单列。分母为零报 not_exercised。三次独立发送逐次报，不投票。\n达标线（提案）\n：\n核心：每一次发送都 kept 且 correct，不带 material 记录问题（v18 验收用的就是\"六篇五发每条都在\"）。\n辅助：三次里至少两次 kept，correct 或 partial。\n不收：模板 0/3 留下（新加坡案件按 D231 改为可选）。\n垃圾：每篇留下的无关陈述不多于 Luna 对照组的中位数（基线，不是目标）。\n候选：§6 例子里该开的一个问题在每次发送都提出，且 settled_by 写得出；不该开的（部署计划、季度指引、同段每行）0 次。\n不达标就读原始答案找机制，再改一处；不在读过的文章上调。\n结构由代码查\n（免费）：schema 通过、引文逐字在原文里、每条一个陈述、候选的 due 在发布日之后。结构错不算内容错，分开报。\n引文检查接受的写法：只在标点、引号、大小写上和原文不同，或在一句之内省略一段，存原文\nD236\n。\n之后的决定对它的影响：参考答案第 2 版里\"整篇什么都不留\"的标注不再当目标\nD240\n；\"垃圾\"一项用定稿金标重算，Luna 基线也重算\nD243 第 12 条\n；第 1 版说的\"§6 例子里该开的一个问题\"是 AI Compute Partnership 的义务金额题；合理的不同判和不同措辞可以接受，可选细节和标签不同不变成硬性的过或不过\nD226、D228\n。\n9.3 迭代：一类一类改\nAlex：\"你一类一类改，快速迭代然后测regression\"。每一轮是下面六步：\n挑最大的一类\n把漏收、误收和被扔的按机制归类计数，从最大的一类开始；报这一类占多少\nAGENTS §1\n。\n找到是哪一句\n读这一类的原始答案，和原文并排（改之前一共至少读过十个原始答案）；对模型做出的选择，接着那次对话问它\"哪一句让你这样判\"；先看请求里有没有它需要的、schema 写不写得出\nAGENTS §1、§6\n。\n只改一处\n一次改一句或一组说同一件事的话，别处删掉同样多；新的话对得上这一页\nAGENTS §6\n。\n全部文章各发一次，和上一版比\n改一句会改变每一次请求，所以要发的是全部文章。\"别的版本都留住、只有这一版丢了\"的核心或辅助才算回归信号；单次发送本身每版有 0–4 条这样的意外丢失\nD243 第 8 条；surprise.py\n。每个信号读原始答案、问模型自己的理由，追到是不是这次改的那一句造成的。这是诊断。出错的那几篇只用来找机制；接不接受，看全部文章三次发送、和新对照一起报的结果\nAGENTS §7\n。\n记一行\n假设、改动、数字（回归先报）、留不留；没用的改动和自己的错也记\nAGENTS §7\n。\n一批改完再验收\n在最终配置上三次发送，按 9.2 由不是作者的读者评，结果给 Alex\n第 1 版 §11\n。\n尺子有疑问就停。\n改的时候发现金标和这一页说法不同、两份标准打架、或读者对\"什么是对的\"有分歧，就停下来先和 Alex 定尺子，不在有疑问的尺子上接着调\nD241；AGENTS §7\n。\n10\nAlex 已经拍过的判例\n第 1 版 §8 的全部，加之后的。\n日期\n判例\n原话或答案\n10-22\n讲话方式\n\"explain and answer like I'm 16 and in human understandable language\"\nAGENTS §8\n观点是什么\nA thesis is an argued view about the business and the world; its stance comes from the argument weighed against what is priced. Financial-statement lines are its evidence, never its definition.\n10-26 D191\n共同量、主题\n决定文字：\"\nindustry_aggregate\nis renamed\nshared_quantity\n: a quantity or price outside any one company that several move on\"，Alex：\"好\"；纯宏观文章只在主题在名单上时开，Alex：\"是\"；主题对公司一对全部，Alex：\"我觉得这个 1 to all mapping 比较 make sense\"\n10-26 D187\n别家公司的量\n选 C（\"可以\"）：任何公司自己的量是那家公司的问题，私营公司也算；记录，未实现\n10-27\n这一步的标准\n\"做一个 hedge fund analyst 的专业视角去看怎么做这一步的标准\"；\"什么叫重要陈述，你也没给例子\"\n10-28 D208\n驱动层\n\"可以，但是不能因为有了这层我们的抽取就是非常的模版化缺少了hedge fund analyst 的灵魂\"；宏观\"算是个global factor to all, 但影响正反面不一定\"\n10-28 D209\n没家的陈述\n\"if it doesn't matter, just fucking throw it away … if there are false positives that got thrown away … you got to pick it up. Again, that's the way to do proper evaluation.\"\n10-28\n不许前视\n\"不是你这么粗暴的把这些都先写好那岂不是lookahead了\"\n10-28\n逐条读\n\"有用的，你得一条一条看是不是真的值得抽才知道你这些改动的抽取效果\"\n10-29 D215\n十一题\n1 A（收入增速 核心）；2 C→D216 \"2028 EPS 预测可以留下来啊，我的意思是因为太久远了，现在才2026\"；3 A（内存短缺 核心）；4 B（AMD 跑分 辅助）；5 C（新加坡案件 不留，D231 改为可选）；6 B（三条政策 辅助）；7 A（云厂商支出 核心）；8 B（Omniverse 辅助）；9 \"辅助属于供给需求\"（Sharon）；10 \"属于产品进度问题\"（NVL72x2）；11 \"一条吧都属于财务\"（Google Cloud 三个数一条）\n10-29 D218\n推断\n\"是推断，你不要再问我了，你要自己尝试 figure out 开始跑\"\n10-29 D220\n谁定标准\n\"你来修正对齐，你是 hedge fund analyst 你来做好标准\"\n10-29 D217\n目标模型\n\"现在开始以haiku 为模型取代luna … 每一轮都根据尺子去迭代 … 小心 overfit … 尺子其实不对 … scope creep\"（D234 起 Haiku 暂停）\n10-29 D226、D228\n评法\n合理的模糊和不同措辞可以接受；金标例子教的是判断逻辑，不是唯一答案；两套既有标准（证据层次、90 题评审）都要用；来源、用途、准入分开判\n10-29\n今天\n\"之前没有对齐一个hedge fund analyst 应该要用什么标准来抽研究观点\"\n10-29 D229\n标准不漂移\n\"1-5 都可以，按照这套标准来开始做测量，这条标准不要漂移\"\n10-29 D231\n新加坡\n\"新加坡那条就随意吧\"\n10-29 D233\n今年\n按公司财年或自然年写都算对\n10-29 D236\n三条提议\n\"好都按照你的提议来。但是你的prompt 改动要确认不是hardcode for nvidia 你在建构的是hedge fund brain\"\n10-29 D237\n通用\n\"It needs to be general purpose as a hedge fund brain\"\n10-29 D238\n直接读\n\"你根本不要再给我跑什么三轮了。每一篇的问题明显地出错的你不要再用工具尺子了，你直接下去看\"\n10-29 D240\n不完整的证据\n\"我不是和你说过不要这么死板吗？这个证据显然是有意义的，不一定什么条件数据时间都要满足，你应该把它记录下来，只是可能档位低一点\"\n10-29 D241\n这份标准\n\"几个大类型的账本 - 开题标准和档次 这两个要完整的定义好写好\"；\"档位根据信息量作定标 -以上有你自己trace 过的golden set 来做为改prompt测试标准\"\n10-29 D243\n签字\n第 5 条：\"这种典型的主张背景属于常识，基本上抽出来也没有太多的意义除非带数字和具体的叙事不然都是属于不采纳\"；方向：\"不是什么事情都是惊弓之鸟需要抽取，杂音还是很大的，我们整体应该倾向谨慎不要看什么都是线索的感觉，但是同时我们要敏感一些新的信息带来的增量不要错过\"；\"其他几条的意见我都ok，sign off\"\n10-29 D242\n这一页\n\"你给我一个网页，这个同时也是你这个环节重要的PRD\"\n10-30 D248\n颗粒度\n七条各选 A（第 6 条\"A， A\"）；原则：\"我觉得核心就是这个颗粒度的把控要符合一个高端的hedge fund analyst 的思维。不要搜集一堆鸡毛蒜皮的事情，要看重要的大事，合并整合关键的信息，这个是这个步骤的原则\"\n11\n在用的标准之间的冲突，怎么摆平\n编号对应盘点报告\ninventory-2026-10-29.md\n的 B1–B20，B21 是后来加的。\"按已拍的\"是已有决定，只是没落实或没写全；文首 12 条的决定记在 D243；\"我定\"只限措辞和记录约定。\n#\n冲突\n处理\n依据\nB1\n共同量的名字和定义：D191 改名并扩到价格；v16 起提示词和代码用\nindustry_aggregate\n、几家公司合计、一个日历年\n按 D191 落实为\nshared_quantity\n，期间仍用自然年；提示词和代码要跟着改\nD191、D243；PRD 06\nB2\n主驱动量含不含产品节点\n含，写成 A、B 两类\nD236（在 D184 之后）\nB3\n约束性义务：一笔或一个计划一题（D210），还是一组入股是一个判断（D201 规则 G）\n按 D210 \"一笔或一个计划\"；一笔交易属不属于已开的计划，由匹配判；同一计划的几种说法是一题；够不上公司规模的单笔交易不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判\nD210、D236、D238、D248\nB4\n开题测试三套，提示词和代码都没用\n第 1 版 §6 四个测试加评审三条写法检查（第 4 节）；测试 4 和库比的部分归匹配\n第 1 版 §6；评审 §1；D211\nB5\nPRD 05 还写着\nfolds_into\n、第 5 块\n抽取不看库；PRD 05 过时段落签字后改指这一页\nD211\nB6\n没家的证据扔不扔\n产品里扔，评估读被扔的、分四种归因；两者不冲突\nD209；第 1 版 §7\nB7\n归档规则三个版本\n以 D207 为准；归档提示里点名 NVIDIA 的那句按 D237 改成通用（后续一步）\nD207、D237\nB8\n分量几级、\"档位\"一词两义\n四级；\"档位\"在这一页叫分量，问题的叫结果档\n第 1 版 §10.3；D243 第 1 条\nB9\n几套\"不要\"的类\n抽取只有一套：按 5.3 各行及其边界判，判为不收的分量是\"不收\"；\"不可用\"属于判断这一步（第 7 节）；\"无关陈述\"用定稿金标计数\n第 1 版 §2、§4；D221；D240；D243 第 12 条\nB10\n股价、目标价、重述、新加坡各处判得不同\n财报后股价反应可选（第 1 版 §10.2）；在研公司的估值倍数、市值、孤零零的目标价可选；重提的过去实际数可选；新加坡可选\n第 1 版 §10.2；D231；D243 第 4、6 条\nB11\n主题怎么连到公司\n一对全部的主题块；文章明写的依赖照记；D208 的通道未建\nD191、D195\nB12\n研究组、驱动层\n研究组不是问题，不代替开题判断；七类驱动是重要性的理由；词汇表只禁 \"thesis driver\"\nD165、D208；评审 §6\nB13\nPRD 06 的重复和替代说法早于 D202、D204\n这一页按 D202、D204 写（第 2 节、5.5）；PRD 06 签字后更新\nD202、D204\nB14\n组成部分算不算同一题\n同一总量的另一种人群或口径是同一题；有自己驱动、答案能分开走、分析员会分别下注的是另一题，要过独立意义测试\nD190；第 1 版 §6；D248\nB15\n标的五档必须叫\"非常看多……\"（代码），还是写业务结果（D186）\n这一页按 D186 写；代码是另一模块，D186 未实现照旧记\nD186\nB16\n转述的公告算事实还是当事人陈述\n事实（role-v29 原文：\"an announcement or completed deal the item reports is a fact even when the reporter names no source\"）；混合的一条按主要说法定层\nPRD 06；D238、D239；D243 第 9 条\nB17\n代码按自然年拒公司明年的财年量\n已修：年限按公司财年数，测试\ntests/test_fiscal_year_horizon.py\nPRD 06 梯子第 5 条；D243\nB18\n\"线索\"\"背景\"一词多义\n\"线索\"只指研究用途的一级（第 7 节）；\"背景\"只指 5.3 那一行\nD221\nB19\n谁把候选并进已开问题\n匹配和碰撞裁决；共同量由代码\nD190、D197、D211\nB20\nAGENTS §8 指向两套标准，D229 又说第 1 版是唯一尺子\n这一页签字后取代第 1 版，AGENTS §8 指向这一页；对两套既有标准（PRD 06 证据层次、90 题评审）的指向保留，它们是这一页的出处\nD228、D229、D243\nB21\nPRD 06 State 写判断要 \"reconsider all evidence still in force\"，D198 之后只显示新到的和依据\n按 D198（在后）；PRD 06 签字后更新\nD198\n12\n校验记录和出处\n三轮独立读者仿真\n每一轮把标准和一组事先定死的案例交给互相看不到答案的读者：作者先写好自己的答案并记 sha256；Claude 的不同模型和 Codex 各读一份。读者只读标准和案例，不读决策、金标、提示词。几方不一致的案例，读原因，追到标准里的一句话，改那一句；下一轮换没见过的读者，加没见过的案例。共 192 个陈述、40 个问题；陈述里 152 个来自测试文章，40 个来自银行、药企、零售、航空、公用事业、矿业、保险等其他行业。\n第三轮，一对读者\n新案例：留不留\n新案例：连分量\n难案例复核：留不留\n新问题\n作者–Codex\n96%\n87%\n84%\n5/8\n作者–Claude sonnet\n94%\n83%\n91%\n6/8\n作者–Claude fable\n93%\n81%\n89%\n6/8\nCodex–sonnet\n94%\n83%\n86%\n7/8\nCodex–fable\n93%\n83%\n84%\n7/8\nsonnet–fable\n98%\n89%\n95%\n8/8\n新案例上留不留的一致率从第二轮的 80–94% 升到 93–98%，连分量从 72–87% 升到 81–89%。剩下的分歧几乎都在核心和辅助的界线，由 D243 第 1 条定下判法。三位读者判对、作者判错的，记作作者的错：最大客户带来的收入和中国销售占比不该开题（独立意义测试），还有两处陈述。记录在\nsimulation-log.md\n。\n十一轮漂移检查\nCodex 只读，逐条对照签过的第 1 版、全部决策、PRD 06，找\"作者自己加的规则\"、和决策矛盾的地方、引错的原话。每一轮的发现都改掉并记下处理。第一轮查出 18 处自己加的规则和 9 处和决策矛盾，比如\"名单外公司的量不开题\"违反 D155、D187，\"抽取要和库里比重复\"违反 D211，把达标线缩短，把\"在谈的金额\"排除出核心。最后三轮只剩措辞层面的问题；第十一轮的发现在 2.14 版改掉，没有再复查。\n36\n2.3\n约20\n2.4\n约10\n2.5\n6\n2.6\n8\n2.7\n7\n2.8\n7\n2.9\n6\n2.10\n5\n2.11\n6\n2.12\n5\n2.13\n被检查的版本；颜色浅的两根是约数\n出处\nPRD 06（Two thesis kinds、Ladder、Four evidence tiers、State、Succession）；PRD 05；第 1 版标准（D229）；\nresearch_notes/对冲基金论点粒度与金标/codex-challenger.md\n§1 八条和 §6、\nindustry-debate-standards.md\n（主分支）；决定 D155、D165、D184、D185、D186、D187、D190、D191、D195、D197、D198、D199、D201、D202、D204、D207、D208、D209、D210、D211、D215、D216、D218、D220、D221、D226、D228、D229、D231、D233、D236、D237、D238、D240、D241、D242。\n文件\n这一页：\ndocs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\n。改它之前先在\ndocs/DECISIONS.md\n记下 Alex 的原话；改完跑一次只读的漂移检查。\n起草过程和证据：\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/\n：\nstandard-v2.md\n到\nstandard-v2.14.md\n、\nsimulation-log.md\n、\nreview-drift-v2.*.md\n、\ninventory-2026-10-29.md\n、\ngolden-v3/\n。\n定稿金标：\ngolden-v3/golden-v3-signed.json\n（sha256 见第 8 节），由\ngolden-v3/finalize.py\n从起草稿定稿。\n第 1 版：\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-analyst-standard/standard.md\n（D229），已由这一页取代，留作出处。\nHivemind · 抽取与开题标准 · 第 5 版 · 2026-10-29 Alex 签字（D243），2026-10-30 按 Alex 逐个选的七条修订（D248），2026-10-31 按 Alex 定的证据四条界线和计数单位修订（D250）。页首的 standard-version 是规则的版本号：改任何一条规则都要升它，金标随之重定，打分程序遇到版本对不上就拒绝打分（D244）。\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; a=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/opening-articles-A2.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False)) for x in a if x[\\\"article\\\"] in [\\\"article O-3 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article 6 of the packet\\\",\\\"article O-4 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-5 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-6 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-7 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-8 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-9 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-10 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-11 (outside the 40)\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; a=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/opening-articles-A2.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False)) for x in a if x[\\\"article\\\"] in [\\\"article O-3 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article 6 of the packet\\\",\\\"article O-4 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-5 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-6 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-7 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-8 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-9 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-10 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-11 (outside the 40)\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"article\": \"article O-3 (outside the 40)\", \"day\": \"2026-09-23\", \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\", \"title\": \"Will Nvidia be allowed to sell more chips to China? This former Trump economic adviser has a hot take.\", \"text\": \"Will Nvidia be allowed to sell more chips to China? This former Trump economic adviser has a hot take.\\nWhen Nvidia ( NVDA ) CEO Jensen Huang arrives at the state dinner on Thursday between President Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping, you better believe he'll be hoping to get a sense of when — and to what extent — his business may resume in China.\\nBoth sides coming to a deal on this front won't be easy, given the politics still at play.\\n\\\"My personal opinion is that we need to have as much competitive advantage here in the United States as we can, because we've got competitive disadvantages against China,\\\" said Gary Cohn, IBM ( IBM ) vice chair and former economic adviser to President Trump in his first term, on Yahoo Finance's Sozzi Unleashed . \\\"You know, they don't have environmental issues. They don't have a cost of capital. They can build data centers wherever they want. The citizens can't stand up and say not in my backyard.\\\"\\nCohn added, \\\"To the extent that the Chinese have all these other significant advantages, the one advantage we have is we've got the chips, and we've got the chip manufacturing here. I'd like to keep that advantage as long as we can. \\\"\\nThe Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China. The problem for Nvidia: The H200 is already an older-generation product, trailing Nvidia's current Blackwell chips in terms of performance.\\nIn other words, Nvidia is likely leaving billions in sales on the table by being unable to get Blackwell chips into China to feed the country's AI startup scene.\\nChinese authorities, however, have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips.\\nWhile Nvidia hasn't factored any sales from China into its outlook for the year, the stock is still up 21% year to date, powered by the AI boom here in the US.\"}\n{\"article\": \"article 6 of the packet\", \"day\": \"2026-09-23\", \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\", \"title\": \"Sandisk vs. NVIDIA: Which AI Stock Is the Better Buy Now?\", \"text\": \"Lately, surging artificial intelligence (AI) and data center demand has benefited both NVIDIA Corporation NVDA and the much smaller Sandisk Corporation SNDK. While the two companies offer different ways to capitalize on the AI boom, which one stands out to be the better buy now? Let's explore –\\nSandisk's AI-Driven Growth Boosts Revenues and Profitability\\nTotal revenues for Sandisk reached $8.97 billion in the fiscal fourth quarter of 2026, up a whopping 372% year over year and 51% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 5 press release.\\nFor fiscal year 2026, revenues reached $20.25 billion, representing 175% year-over-year growth. The Data Center segment has been the major growth driver, with revenues jumping 437% as AI and data center demand increased the need for high-value storage.\\nIn the fourth fiscal quarter, revenue growth came more from higher pricing than higher volumes. As a result, gross margins rose to 84.6% compared to 26.2% a year ago.\\nFor fiscal year 2026, gross margin was 71.5% compared to 30.1% a year earlier. Profitability also improved dramatically, with GAAP net income coming in at $11.43 billion versus a loss of $1.64 billion in the previous year.\\nThe growth doesn't seem to be temporary, as management remains bullish on their near-term outlook. They expect revenues to be between $10.3 billion and $10.8 billion in the fiscal first quarter of 2027, which would mean another strong quarterly growth following a huge fiscal fourth quarter. Now that the company has signed five more New Business Model agreements, revenue growth and profitability will surely improve.\\nNVIDIA's AI Demand Drives Strong Growth and Margin Expansion\\nThe Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\\nNVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago. Strong operating performance helped NVIDIA convert robust revenue growth into solid earnings growth.\\nNVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027. At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise. Additionally, the next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning NVIDIA to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment.\\nBoth Sandisk and NVIDIA have delivered exceptional earnings growth, fueled by an increase in AI and data center demand, resulting in sharp revenue gains and substantial margin expansion. Both companies also look quite confident about their near-term outlooks.\\nHowever, Sandisk is in a highly cyclical memory business. If supply catches up with demand, memory prices may come under pressure and weigh on margins. Moreover, the company's fast-paced growth has raised expectations, which has made its growth trajectory susceptible if pricing momentum weakens.\\nSince Sandisk's business is dependent on storage demand, it has created greater concentration risk. In contrast, NVIDIA is capitalizing on the broader AI ecosystem, has dominant data center exposure, and continues to deliver exceptional AI-driven growth. To top it off, NVIDIA's 63.7% net profit margin compared with Sandisk's 56.5%, indicates its greater efficiency in converting revenues into bottom-line profits.\\nTaken together, these factors make NVIDIA a better buy than Sandisk at the moment. NVIDIA currently has a Zacks Rank #1 (Strong Buy), while Sandisk has a Zacks Rank #3 (Hold). You can see the complete list of today's Zacks Rank #1 stocks here.\"}\n{\"article\": \"article O-4 (outside the 40)\", \"day\": \"2026-09-23\", \"publisher\": \"fool.com\", \"title\": \"I've Held Nvidia for Nearly 3 Years. Here's Whether I'd Still Buy Today.\", \"text\": \"Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has been one of the best stocks to own in the artificial intelligence (AI) arms race. It is up nearly 1,500% since 2023 began, providing investors with incredible returns. Unfortunately, I do not have those returns in my portfolio.\\nI bought the stock at the beginning of 2023 and watched it double. Then I sold the stock because I was worried that AI computing demand wouldn't last. I was very wrong. I had to disregard all of the prior gains and purchase shares in 2024 after it had run up some more, which has still been a great investment for me.\\nBut would I buy the stock again today? You bet I would.\\nNvidia is still a dominant force in the industry\\nNvidia is turning the investment world on its head. Normally, the law of large numbers dictates that the larger a company is, the harder it is to grow. Nvidia is the world's largest company and has reached heights that no other company has ever achieved. However, it's growing faster than any other stock among the world's top 10 companies. That's hard to believe, but it showcases just how massive the demand is for AI computing power.\\nNvidia makes GPUs , which are the go-to computing unit for all sorts of AI operations, be it training or inference. While there are alternatives, the Nvidia ecosystem cannot be beaten. Additionally, custom AI chips are starting to become more popular among many AI hyperscalers. Still, there will always be a place for computing units like Nvidia's GPUs that are flexible and can handle a wide variety of workloads.\\nDue to Nvidia's market position, it has advanced knowledge of how much computing power the AI hyperscalers are planning. The AI companies are making multiyear plans to ensure they have mapped out when they will need computing units, because data centers don't go up overnight. This gives Nvidia the ability to issue long-term guidance, and the guidance regarding the AI build-out is simply incredible.\\nNvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030. Considering the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year, that's huge growth still to come.\\nSo, if you think you've missed the boat on Nvidia, think again. There is still plenty of upside to come, and Nvidia will be one of the biggest beneficiaries of the AI build-out. However, none of it is priced into Nvidia's stock.\\nThat's pretty much in line with where other big tech stocks trade when one-time effects don't skew their valuations, but there's one problem: Nvidia is growing far faster than any of its big tech peers. The trailing price-to-earnings ratio doesn't account for Nvidia's massive growth ahead, and that's where a forward-looking metric helps out.\\nAt less than 15 times next year's earnings, Nvidia's stock has almost no growth priced into it today. That means the stock has major upside over the next year. Furthermore, with the AI build-out expected to intensify through 2030, that leaves plenty more room for Nvidia to run.\\nEven if you've missed out on Nvidia so far, now is the perfect time to buy more. Very little growth has been priced in, and Nvidia's stock looks well positioned to be one of the best investments over the next few years.\"}\n{\"article\": \"article O-5 (outside the 40)\", \"day\": \"2026-09-25\", \"publisher\": \"fool.com\", \"title\": \"Credo vs. Nvidia: Which AI Chip Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026?\", \"text\": \"The artificial intelligence boom has transformed the semiconductor landscape. Deciding between a high-growth connectivity specialist like Credo Technology Group ( CRDO +5.75% ) and the industry giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) requires a look at their specific niches.\\nBoth companies are central to modern data centers, but they solve different problems. Credo provides the high-speed wiring and connectivity chips that keep data moving, while Nvidia supplies the massive processing power AI models require. Comparing these two reveals how different players profit from the same infrastructure trend.\\nCredo designs high-speed copper and optical interconnect solutions essential for modern AI-driven infrastructure. The company operates as a niche provider of high-speed connectivity solutions among semiconductor stocks serving AI data centers. Credo relies on a limited number of customers for a substantial portion of its revenue, with sales to its top 10 customers accounting for approximately 90% of total revenue in fiscal 2026. This level of customer concentration adds risk to the business. The company also maintains a partnership with Oracle ( ORCL -0.13% ) to develop ZeroFlap optics to improve reliability in AI data centers.\\nFinancial performance has accelerated significantly recently. In fiscal 2026, which ended in May 2026, revenue reached nearly $1.3 billion, up roughly 205.7% from the previous year. This growth helped the company achieve a net income of approximately $472.3 million. The net margin for the period was approximately 35.4%, a substantial improvement from the 11.9% net margin reported in the prior fiscal year.\\nThe balance sheet remains highly liquid. As of its May 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio (a measure of short-term debt-paying ability) is nearly 10.2x. The debt-to-equity ratio (total debt compared to equity) is 0.0x, indicating no debt relative to equity. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) was nearly $407.0 million. Stock-based compensation represented roughly 39.3% of operating cash flow, which inflates reported cash generation because SBC is a non-cash expense added back in the cash flow statement.\\nNvidia is the dominant provider of GPU-accelerated computing platforms used in gaming, professional visualization, and data centers. The company sells its products to massive cloud service providers and AI model makers globally. Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds a layer of risk to the business. The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\\nRevenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\\nStrong profitability supports a robust capital structure. As of its January 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio is approximately 3.9x. The debt-to-equity ratio (which compares total debt to shareholder equity) is under 0.1x, meaning the company carries very little debt. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) reached nearly $96.7 billion for the fiscal year. This massive cash generation provides significant flexibility for research, development, and strategic acquisitions.\\nCredo faces intense competition from larger, well-established semiconductor companies and potential new entrants in the data infrastructure market. The company depends heavily on a small number of customers, making it vulnerable to losses or shifts in purchasing patterns. Its business model relies on third-party manufacturing partners, exposing the company to supply chain disruptions and geopolitical risks regarding operations in Taiwan and mainland China. Furthermore, evolving international trade controls and export restrictions could restrict its ability to serve key markets.\\nNvidia faces significant risks from global geopolitical tensions and export control regulations, particularly for high-performance AI chips. These restrictions have impacted sales to China and could continue to limit market access. The company faces intense competition from other hardware suppliers and internal development efforts by customers like Microsoft ( MSFT +1.22% ) , Amazon ( AMZN +0.51% ) , and Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.61% ) ( GOOG -0.77% ) . Nvidia also manages various legal challenges, including inquiries from competition regulators worldwide regarding its market practices.\\nNvidia trades at a lower forward P/E based on future earnings estimates, while both carry a high P/S ratio compared to historical averages.\\nI'd buy Nvidia, and it isn't close. Here's why:\\nCredo's growth is not the issue. Revenue in its latest quarter more than doubled from a year earlier, and management guided the current quarter higher still. The stock fell anyway because gross margin guidance came in flat, and shares now sit well below their 52-week high. That's the problem with a company that gets about 90% of its revenue from 10 customers. Any hint of slower spending from one of them moves the stock, and the valuation leaves no room for a miss.\\nNvidia is growing nearly as fast at a scale that makes Credo look like a rounding error. Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all. Nvidia has its own concentration risk, with two customers making up more than a third of sales, and the Hugging Face deal shows it's willing to spend to stay ahead.\\nFor a long-term investor, Nvidia offers the same AI infrastructure exposure at a lower price per dollar of expected earnings, with far less dependence on any single buyer. Credo belongs in a portfolio only as a small position you can afford to watch swing.\\nCredo more than tripled revenue last year but leans on 10 customers for 90% of sales, while Nvidia is doubling revenue at a scale nobody else in AI hardware can match.\\nBy Mike Schwenk – Updated Sep 25, 2026 at 3:00PM EST\\nCredo makes the high-speed connectivity chips and cables that move data between servers inside AI data centers, and revenue more than tripled in fiscal 2026.\\nNvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\\nBoth companies depend on a small group of customers, but Nvidia's scale, margins, and lower valuation make it the stronger buy today.\"}\n{\"article\": \"article O-6 (outside the 40)\", \"day\": \"2026-09-27\", \"publisher\": \"bloomberg.com\", \"title\": \"China May Let Alibaba Buy New Nvidia Chips, The Information Says\", \"text\": \"(Bloomberg) -- The Chinese government has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd to purchase Nvidia Corp.'s new RTX Pro 5500 chips, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter.\\nEllison Pledges $9.2 Billion More in Oracle Shares for Loans\\nMcDonald's Selloff Hits 30% as Big Mac Inflation Spurs Pushback\\nThe Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked some companies to report on their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processor chip, which was released this month, including how many of them and what they would use the product for, the report said, adding that the ministry told some of the firms it intends to approve the purchases.\\nNvidia has faced export restrictions from the US as well as curbs from Beijing over the sale of its chips to China, due to a web of bilateral tensions ranging from security to competition on artificial intelligence.\\nThe US administration recently lifted restrictions on some Nvidia chips, though the products tend to be older-generation chips that have been sold in small quantities.\\nGLP-1 Users Are Furious After Employers Drop Insurance Benefits\\nThis $2 Billion Company Dresses HBO's The Pitt Cast and Real Doctors Too\"}\n{\"article\": \"article O-7 (outside the 40)\", \"day\": \"2026-09-22\", \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\", \"title\": \"NVIDIA Corporation (NVDA) Is a Trending Stock: Facts to Know Before Betting on It\", \"text\": \"Shares of this maker of graphics chips for gaming and artificial intelligence have returned +9.1% over the past month versus the Zacks S&P 500 composite's +1.3% change. The Zacks Semiconductor - General industry, to which Nvidia belongs, has gained 8% over this period. Now the key question is: Where could the stock be headed in the near term?\\nAlthough media reports or rumors about a significant change in a company's business prospects usually cause its stock to trend and lead to an immediate price change, there are always certain fundamental factors that ultimately drive the buy-and-hold decision.\\nRather than focusing on anything else, we at Zacks prioritize evaluating the change in a company's earnings projection. This is because we believe the fair value for its stock is determined by the present value of its future stream of earnings.\\nOur analysis is essentially based on how sell-side analysts covering the stock are revising their earnings estimates to take the latest business trends into account. When earnings estimates for a company go up, the fair value for its stock goes up as well. And when a stock's fair value is higher than its current market price, investors tend to buy the stock, resulting in its price moving upward. Because of this, empirical studies indicate a strong correlation between trends in earnings estimate revisions and short-term stock price movements.\\nFor the current quarter, Nvidia is expected to post earnings of $2.47 per share, indicating a change of +90% from the year-ago quarter. The Zacks Consensus Estimate has changed +5.8% over the last 30 days.\\nFor the current fiscal year, the consensus earnings estimate of $9.25 points to a change of +93.9% from the prior year. Over the last 30 days, this estimate has changed +5.1%.\\nFor the next fiscal year, the consensus earnings estimate of $15.33 indicates a change of +65.8% from what Nvidia is expected to report a year ago. Over the past month, the estimate has changed +21.8%.\\nWhile earnings growth is arguably the most superior indicator of a company's financial health, nothing happens as such if a business isn't able to grow its revenues. After all, it's nearly impossible for a company to increase its earnings for an extended period without increasing its revenues. So, it's important to know a company's potential revenue growth.\\nFor Nvidia, the consensus sales estimate for the current quarter of $109.02 billion indicates a year-over-year change of +91.2%. For the current and next fiscal years, $406.05 billion and $671.95 billion estimates indicate +88% and +65.5% changes, respectively.\\nNo investment decision can be efficient without considering a stock's valuation. Whether a stock's current price rightly reflects the intrinsic value of the underlying business and the company's growth prospects is an essential determinant of its future price performance.\\nWhile comparing the current values of a company's valuation multiples, such as price-to-earnings (P/E), price-to-sales (P/S), and price-to-cash flow (P/CF), with its own historical values helps determine whether its stock is fairly valued, overvalued, or undervalued, comparing the company relative to its peers on these parameters gives a good sense of the reasonability of the stock's price.\\nThe Zacks Value Style Score (part of the Zacks Style Scores system), which pays close attention to both traditional and unconventional valuation metrics to grade stocks from A to F (an A is better than a B; a B is better than a C; and so on), is pretty helpful in identifying whether a stock is overvalued, rightly valued, or temporarily undervalued.\"}\n{\"article\": \"article O-8 (outside the 40)\", \"day\": \"2026-09-22\", \"publisher\": \"gurufocus.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Gains as Apple Pulls AI Off the Meter\", \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ), the AI-accelerator and data-center platform leader, faced a fresh edge-computing test as Apple ( NASDAQ:AAPL ) pushed its new Macs as machines capable of running heavyweight AI privately instead of constantly calling the cloud. Nvidia shares gained approximately 0.7% to $229.08, suggesting investors were hardly treating Apple's move as an immediate threat to the GPU giant's core data-center engine.\\nWarning! GuruFocus has detected 3 Warning Sign with MSFT.\\nApple showed four Mac Studios working together to run a trillion-parameter model, using shared memory across the machines to tackle workloads once associated almost entirely with expensive server infrastructure. That is an eye-catching demonstration, but the scale gap remains enormous: Nvidia's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion overall, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI. The GuruFocus picture reinforces that underlying strength, with Nvidia carrying a GF Score of 95/100 and particularly strong readings for profitability, growth and financial strength, while GF Value remains the noticeably weaker part of the profile.\\nThe bigger question is not whether a handful of Macs can run a giant model. They clearly can. The question is how much inference, coding and repetitive enterprise AI work permanently shifts from cloud infrastructure onto local machines. Apple could chip away at some private and predictable workloads, especially where data security and recurring token costs matter. But hyperscale training, massive networking demands and unpredictable peak workloads still play directly into Nvidia's territory. For Nvidia investors, the real risk starts only if local AI becomes a structural substitute for cloud inference rather than another layer of the computing stack.\"}\n{\"article\": \"article O-9 (outside the 40)\", \"day\": \"2026-09-22\", \"publisher\": \"barchart.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock is Historically Cheap. The Decade-Low Multiple Reflects Risks, But This Looks Like a Can't-Miss Buy Opportunity for NVDA.\", \"text\": \"Nvidia Stock is Historically Cheap. The Decade-Low Multiple Reflects Risks, But This Looks Like a Can't-Miss Buy Opportunity for NVDA.\\nNvidia (NVDA) stock is currently trading at less than 17 times expected forward 12-month earnings, representing the cheapest valuation the shares have commanded in over a decade and roughly half the multiple investors were willing to pay as recently as early 2025.\\nThis dramatic de-rating has occurred against a backdrop of accelerating, not decelerating, fundamentals — a paradox that sits at the heart of the debate over whether NVDA's compressed multiple signals heightened risk or a rare value buying opportunity.\\nSpaceX Has Huge, Unusual Put Options Volume - Is SPCX Set to Fall or Rise From Here?\\nLucid Just Struck a Partnership With Bolt on Mobility. What This Means for LCID Stock.\\nThe disconnect between Nvidia's soaring earnings power and its shrinking valuation multiple suggests the market is pricing in meaningful skepticism about the sustainability of the current AI spending cycle.\\nThe fundamental case for Nvidia remains extraordinarily strong. Fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue surging 117% to $89 billion, and management has guided for approximately $108 billion in third-quarter revenue, implying 89% growth.\\nCEO Jensen Huang has projected that chip sales could double in calendar year 2027, while CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled. There has never been a company of Nvidia's scale growing at anything close to this rate.\\nYet several legitimate risks explain the market's caution. Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\\nOther tangible headwinds include calls from leading artificial intelligence (AI) labs, including Anthropic and OpenAI, to slow frontier development; rising community backlash against data center construction; soaring interest rates that pressure hyperscaler capital budgets; and Nvidia's complete exclusion from the Chinese data center market.\\nAdditionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\\nThe valuation math, however, is difficult to ignore. At 14 times next fiscal year's consensus earnings estimate, Nvidia trades at a fraction of its 10-year average price/earnings (P/E) ratio of 61 – and well below semiconductor peers like Advanced Micro Devices (AMD) at over 200 times trailing earnings and Broadcom (AVGO) at 46 times.\\nAnalysts remain overwhelmingly bullish, with 45 of 50 firms rating the stock a \\\"Strong Buy,\\\" a mean price target near $326 implying 42% upside from current levels, and earnings estimates that have been revised sharply higher — from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029 — over just the past nine months.\\nIf Nvidia simply maintains its current trailing P/E of 28 while delivering on consensus earnings projections, the stock would roughly double over the next 12 to 18 months.\\nThe decade-low multiple is not so much a red flag as it is the market's attempt to reconcile unprecedented growth with unprecedented uncertainty about how long that growth can last. For investors with a multi-year horizon and a tolerance for volatility tied to AI spending cycles, the risk-reward profile at current levels tilts decisively toward opportunity rather than warning.\\nThis article was created with the support of automated content tools from our partners at Sigma.AI. Together, our financial data and AI solutions help us to deliver more informed market headline analysis to readers faster than ever.\"}\n{\"article\": \"article O-10 (outside the 40)\", \"day\": \"2026-09-23\", \"publisher\": \"fool.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's Demand Is Outpacing Its 70% Growth Forecast, and That Is Just the Beginning\", \"text\": \"There may be no better company to represent the AI boom than Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA). Its graphics processing units (GPUs) are the backbone of artificial intelligence data centers, and in just three years, the company's market cap has skyrocketed by 445% to $5.5 trillion, making it the most valuable company in the world .\\nWhat continues to impress many investors is that Nvidia isn't done growing yet. In its fiscal 2027 second-quarter report, Management forecast that in fiscal 2028, its sales will rise by 70%, thanks to continued demand for AI chips.\\nBut what's really interesting is that Nvidia leadership said it could double its sales in fiscal 2028 if it were able to overcome the constraints on the supply of commodities needed to produce its hardware.\\nIn short, demand for Nvidia's chips is far outpacing the company's own forecast sales grown. Here's what's happening and why it's good news for Nvidia investors.\\nOn Nvidia's second-quarter fiscal 2027 earnings call , Nvidia CFO Colette Kress said:\\nIncredibly, we are seeing demand acceleration even at our scale. Customer forecasts point to our growth doubling next year. However, as I mentioned earlier, we expect to grow approximately 70% as we are supply-constrained.\\nKress is absolutely right. This level of sales growth is incredible for a $5.5 trillion company. To put it in perspective, Apple 's market cap is just under $5 trillion, and its revenue growth was 16% in the third quarter to $109 billion. Still impressive for its size, but nowhere near Nvidia's growth.\\nThere are a handful of reasons why demand for artificial intelligence processors remains high, the first of which is that agentic AI is replacing human-generated prompts as the main driver of workload growth. In short, AI is doing more tasks on its own, and that's driving computing needs much higher.\\nNvidia CEO Jensen Huang said on the earnings call that \\\"the amount of compute necessary is just extraordinary\\\" because AI agents use between 15 and 100 times as much compute as would be required with a human guiding the work. All of this is combining to boost demand for Nvidia's GPUs.\\nHuang's not the only one projecting far more spending on AI infrastructure. S&P Global recently forecast that tech companies will spend an estimated $1.3 trillion on AI next year.\\nSometimes it can be a problem when a company can't meet its customers' demand. But in this case, it's not so much of a problem because the entire tech industry -- including nearly all hardware makers -- is struggling to keep up.\\nFor example, Micron Technology 's memory chips are having a significant problem, with demand far outpacing supply. That's led to skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron, and global supply constraints.\\nThe big picture for Nvidia investors is that agentic AI could drive GPU demand much higher than it is now and will likely continue to boost Nvidia's sales for years to come. As Huang noted on the call:\\nToday, the vast majority of AI is prompted by people. I believe that this last month, it has crossed. Most AI are now agentic. But in the future, every company will have a whole bunch of agents.\\nNvidia says it's working to increase supply to meet customer demand. If it succeeds, it could double its sales in fiscal 2028. But even if it doesn't, Nvidia appears to be on the cusp of another surge in processor demand that could last for years.\"}\n{\"article\": \"article O-11 (outside the 40)\", \"day\": \"2026-09-24\", \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\", \"title\": \"Marvell vs. NVIDIA: One AI Stock Looks Like the Better Buy Now\", \"text\": \"Both Marvell Technology, Inc. MRVL and its larger counterpart, NVIDIA Corporation NVDA, are benefiting from the rising Data Center demand amid the ongoing artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure boom. While both companies delivered strong quarterly results, differences in profitability, AI exposure, and outlook warrant a closer look when determining which stock to buy now.\\nMarvell's AI Momentum Powers Record Growth\\nGiven Marvell's scale, the company posted substantial revenue growth in its latest quarterly results. Revenues for the fiscal second quarter of 2027 reached a record $2.739 billion, up 37% year over year, according to the company's Aug. 27 press release. With Marvell supplying vital infrastructure for data center systems, its Data Center revenues grew even faster, up 46% year over year.\\nFor the fiscal third quarter of 2027, management expects revenues to reach $3.15 billion, plus or minus 5%. At the midpoint, it represents roughly 15% sequential growth. Management also remained optimistic about revenue growth throughout fiscal year 2027 and 2028. This suggests that Marvell's strong performance goes beyond the latest quarter, with management increasing its forward expectations.\\nMarvell's Chairman and chief executive officer, Matt Murphy, said the company is seeing exceptionally strong AI-related bookings, with growth expected to accelerate further across their Data Center portfolio, supported by robust demand in Connectivity and accelerating Custom business.\\nNVIDIA's AI Boom Fuels Record Growth and Strong Profitability\\nNVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release. Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\\nNVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\\nNVIDIA expects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, for the fiscal third quarter of 2027, indicating continued sequential growth. Profitability is also expected to remain strong, while the company's Vera Rubin platform is now in full production, positioning NVIDIA to capitalize on continued AI infrastructure demand.\\nBanking on AI and Data Center demand, both Marvell and NVIDIA delivered strong quarterly results. Still, their exposure to the AI opportunity varies significantly.\\nMarvell's growth is increasingly driven by strong data center demand. However, if capital spending by AI infrastructure companies slows down in the future, Marvell's growth could decelerate. In contrast, NVIDIA's exposure across the broader AI computing platform, along with significantly faster Data Center growth, provides greater diversification within the AI market and reduces the company's exposure to concentration risk.\\nMarvell also appears to be emphasizing revenue expansion, but its forecasted fiscal third-quarter 2027 non-GAAP gross margin of 57.5-58.5% is below the reported 58.9% in the fiscal second quarter. This suggests that the expected revenue increase may not translate into proportional earnings, a concern for investors to monitor. In comparison, NVIDIA expects a non-GAAP gross margin of 74% in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, highlighting its much stronger margin profile.\\nMoreover, NVIDIA appears more efficient than Marvell at generating profits from shareholders' equity. This is because NVIDIA's return on equity (ROE) of 96% exceeds Marvell's ROE of 17.9%.\\nTherefore, NVIDIA's stronger growth, profitability, and ROE make it a more compelling investment option now than Marvell. Additionally, NVIDIA appears more attractively valued than Marvell. Per the price/earnings ratio, NVDA trades at 24.39 forward earnings compared with MRVL's forward earnings multiple of 62.25.\\nNVIDIA currently has a Zacks Rank #1 (Strong Buy), while Marvell has a Zacks Rank #3 (Hold). You can see the complete list of today's Zacks Rank #1 stocks here .\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from bs4 import BeautifulSoup; s=BeautifulSoup(open(\\\"docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\\\").read(),\\\"html.parser\\\"); t=s.get_text(\\\"\\\\n\\\",strip=True); a=t.index(\\\"3\\\\n问题的五类：\\\"); b=t.index(\\\"5\\\\n证据\\\",a); print(t[a:b])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from bs4 import BeautifulSoup; s=BeautifulSoup(open(\\\"docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\\\").read(),\\\"html.parser\\\"); t=s.get_text(\\\"\\\\n\\\",strip=True); a=t.index(\\\"3\\\\n问题的五类：\\\"); b=t.index(\\\"5\\\\n证据\\\",a); print(t[a:b])'\"","aggregated_output":"3\n问题的五类：账本的几大类型\n开题理由是 D184 定的四类，D236 把主驱动量扩到产品节点，所以写成五类。判两件事，互不顶替：这是谁的问题（标的还是主题）；为什么值得开（开题理由）。\n类\n开题理由\n标的或主题\n问什么\n量还是事件\n期间和到期\n核对材料\nsettled_by\n出处\nA\n主驱动量\nmain_driver\n标的\n承载公司价值的那个年度量，不管公司指不指引：撑起业务的分部收入、毛利率、资本开支；跨行业如银行的净利息收入、零售商的同店销售、公用事业的费率基数、航空公司的燃油成本（当它是争论焦点时）。不是每一行报表数\n量，五档\n公司财年的今年或明年；到期日是这个数的发布日\n公布这个数的公开材料，通常是年报\nD184：\nsettled_by\n是 \"the public material that resolves it\"；PRD 06 梯子第 5 条\nB\n主产品节点\nmain_driver\n标的\n承载公司价值的那一代产品哪天走到能带来收入的那一步（通常是开始出货）；发布、送样是它的证据，只有会分别下注的里程碑（新药的三期数据和获批）各一题\nD248 第 3 条\n事件，五档，或是非两档\nPRD 06 梯子第 1 条\n能判出结果的那天\n公司公告、发货确认\nD236\nC\n约束性义务\nbinding_obligation\n标的\n公司以自己名义承担的：为别人的债务、租约或收入担保或提供信用支持；借钱给别人；承诺购买（向别人买产能或服务也算）；为客户的产能或收入兜底；或写明规模、尚未完成的入股或收购\nD236；收购按 D243 第 7 条\n。规模要大到能改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险才开；够不上的单笔入股、收购、租约、授信不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判\nD248 第 6 条\n。不算：自己借钱、自己的租约、已完成或没写规模的投资。D239 选定的 role-v29 原文：\"Its own borrowing, its own leases and a completed or unsized investment are not obligations\"；D238 的记录写作 \"the company's own borrowing and leases and a completed or unsized stake do not\"\n还没签：是非两档。已经承担（一笔或一个计划）：金额，五档\n检查日最多在文章后 18 个月\nD185\n；已承担的按下一份年报\n签约公告、年报承诺附注\nD184、D185、D210、D236、D238、D243\nD\n共同量\nshared_quantity\nD191、D243 第 2 条\n；现行提示词和代码还叫\nindustry_aggregate\n，按这一页改\n主题\n任何一家公司之外、好几家都会受它影响的量或价格：几家公司的合计（云厂商资本开支）、价格（内存合约价、油价）、利率。同一总量的另一种人群、另一种说法是这个量的证据\n量，五档\n自然年的今年或明年。同一个量、同一年只有一个问题，由代码按\"主题＋量＋期间\"判\nD197\n；量的类别是\nother\n的不算\n几家公司年报、统计发布、官方决定\nD184、D191、D197、D243；PRD 06\nE\n政策阶段\npolicy_stage\n问的是一家公司的收入、利润、份额或产品进度，算标的，即使由政策驱动；问的是世界上的事（许可制度、利率、合计支出），算主题。D184 采纳的 role-v12 原文：\"A question about a company's revenue, profit, share or product progress is an asset thesis; put its ticker in object, even when policy or macroeconomics drives it\"\n一个政策或监管过程到了哪一步，写明谁说了算\n事件，五档或两档\n能判出那一步的那天。文章没写日期的待决监管事项，用本财年末（role-v12 原文：\"A pending regulatory decision may lack a date; use the current fiscal year-end as its due date\"）；这个日期是研究员定的检查日，要标明\n评审 §1 第 1 条\n官方决定、公报\nD184\n例子（跨行业）：\nA：\nNVIDIA FY2027 数据中心收入；一家地区银行 FY2026 净利息收入；一家公用事业公司 2027 年费率基数。\nB：\n下一代机架系统能否在 2027 年底前开始出货；一家车企的新平台能否在 2026 年内开始交付。\nC：\nNVIDIA 为云客户产能兜底的义务在 FY2027 年报达到多少；一家保险公司是否在 2027 年中前为合资公司 5 亿美元债务签署担保。\nD：\n五家最大云厂商 2026 年合计资本开支；2027 年 DRAM 合约价相对 2026 年的变化、美联储 2026 年最后一次会议后的利率上限。\nE：\n中国是否在 2027-01-31 前正式批准本国公司进口 H200；美国是否在某日前对某类芯片实行出口许可。这两题问的是许可这件世界上的事，所以是主题，role-v12 原文的例子就是 \"licensing\"。一个监管决定对一家公司收入或产品进度的影响，问成那家公司的量或产品节点时，是标的问题（同一句原文）。\n五类之外的东西去哪\n七类驱动不是问题类型。\n它们是一条陈述为什么重要的理由，用来判分量和归档。D208 设计的\"每家公司种下的驱动问题\"由分析员开始覆盖时写好，不从文章里开；还没建。\n别家公司自己的量\n（对手的份额、客户的资本开支）：D187 定了\"任何公司自己的量是那家公司的问题，私营公司也算\"，但还没实现，要先在覆盖名单里加私营公司这一类。D155 定了\"公司清单可以作为初始参考，但清单外不能忽略\"。现行代码在匹配时跳过名单外的对象\nPRD 06 Step 4\n。所以抽取照常可以提这样的问题，它要过第 4 节的测试；名单外的现在会被匹配跳过，这不算抽取错。\n宏观量\n（利率、通胀）：按 D 类共同量开题\nD243 第 2 条\n。只来自一篇纯宏观文章时，只有主题在名单上才开\nD191 第 3 条\n。宏观对每家公司的正负，在判断那家公司时读主题块决定\nD191 第 1 条、D195\n。\n开题理由是封闭的\nD184\n：一件事属不属于五类之一，按上表逐条看，比如一个监管决定看它是不是 E 类。哪一类都不属于的不开；它是不是证据、挂到哪，照第 5 节和归档规则。\n问题怎么写：PRD 06 的梯子规则，不重写\n一个问题只问一个量的一个期间，或一件事；能各自朝相反方向走、而且分析员会分别下注的部分要分开\nPRD 06 梯子第 3 条；\"分别下注\"是 D248 加的，PRD 06 签字后更新\n，分开的每一题都要过第 4 节的测试。\n写成问句，不写成断言\nPRD 06 Rationale\n。\n结果档可核对、不重叠、不留缝，到期恰好一档成立；中间一档写明和什么比；\"小幅\"\"部分\"这种不能核对的说法不用，部分状态写成单独一档\nPRD 06 梯子第 2 条\n。标的问题的档描述业务结果；主题问题的档只写结局，不写好坏\nPRD 06、D186\n。\n到期日：量是它的发布日；事件是能判出结果的那天。公司的量用公司财年，别的用自然年，只开今年和明年\nPRD 06 梯子第 5 条；约束性义务例外，D185\n。\n4\n开题标准\n一个问题值得单独追，要同时满足三件事：它的答案可能改变仓位；能在一个日期用公开材料判出落在哪一档；库里没有问题在问同一件事。\n开多少个是判断的结果，不是目标\n第 1 版 §6；评审 §1\n。\n颗粒度按一个高端对冲基金分析员：只有会单独下注的大事开题，鸡毛蒜皮不开题（它的陈述留不留照第 5 节判）；同一个赌注的几种说法是一题，截止日、里程碑、财年和日历年各按本节\"同一题、续期，还是另一题\"里的那一条判\nD248\n。\n另有一条前提：新问题要有独立的投资判断；一个新产品、一个新数字、一个更窄的范围，单凭这些不开\nD201\n。\n四个测试\n第 1 版 §6\n值得追\n写得出\"这个结果会改变哪项收入、利润、现金或信用风险、竞争位置\"吗？写不出就留作证据，不开题。\"数字很大\"不算理由。单笔交易、产品、项目，规模够不上改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险的，不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判\nD248 第 6 条：问的就是小交易、小产品\n；这一测试的其余理由（包括竞争位置）不变。\n独立意义\n能写出两个情景吗：其他问题的答案不变，只有这一题相反，而对业务的判断确实不同？写不出就优先作为证据\n评审 §1 第 3、8 条\n。评审里的一个判例：NVIDIA FY2027 总收入题判为数据中心收入题的证据，理由是\"该段明确说主要增长来自数据中心，未提出独立的其他业务争论\"。这是对那一段材料的判断，别的材料要逐个判。\n能核对\n有一个日期和一份公开材料能把答案放进事先固定的档吗？没有就重写问题。到期没有材料记\"未能判定\"，不算错。\n不是重复\n和已有问题比，只是预测值、措辞、来源、方向或同一总量的另一种口径不同，最终核对的是同一个结果吗？是就是同一题。抽取看不到库\nD211\n，这一条在抽取时只对同一篇文章里提出的几个问题判；和库里的比由匹配做。\n另外三条写法检查\n评审 §1 第 1、5、6 条\n。没过的问题不能照原样开，要重写；重写不了就不开，材料作证据。\n原文支持：\n主体、量或事件、时间依据都能在原文找到。原文没给日期、而规则允许研究员定检查日的，用那个日期并标明：没写日期的待决监管事项用本财年末（第 3 节 E 类）；还没签的约束性义务用文章后 18 个月以内能核对签约的那天\nD185、D210\n。不把\"拟议\"\"预计\"写成\"已签\"\"已完成\"。\n口径完整：\n算谁、不算谁、算什么写清楚；支出、融资、担保、订单、收入不混。\n时间正确：\n原文支持这个期间，不把几年的平均写成今年。\n不开的\n第 1 版 §6 的清单\n不开\n出处\n它变成什么\n季度数字和季度指引\n第 1 版 §6；PRD 06 梯子第 6 条\n证据\n同一段指引里的每一行（\"费用率、税率是模型输入，不是争论\"）\n第 1 版 §6\n证据\n没日期的\"护城河\"\"战略\"\n第 1 版 §6\n证据\n高管任免、股东投票、回购授权；章程\n第 1 版 §6；章程出自 D239 选定的 role-v29 原文（\"executive appointments, shareholder votes or bylaws\"）\n证据或不留\n例行诉讼程序\n第 1 版 §6\n证据\n客户的部署计划（\"它是供应商需求和产品问题的证据，除非改变供应商自己的现金或信用风险\"）\n第 1 版 §6\n证据；改变公司自己的现金或信用风险时按 C 类判\n以约束性义务为理由开：公司自己借钱、自己的租约、已完成或没写规模的投资。它们不是约束性义务；对别的问题仍可以是证据，或按别的开题理由判\nD236、D238\n证据\n把远期年度预测改装成事件\n第 1 版 §6\n证据\n后年及以后的年度量（约束性义务除外）\nPRD 06 梯子第 5 条；D18\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; a=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/opening-articles-A2.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False)) for x in a if x[\\\"article\\\"] in [\\\"article O-5 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-2 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-4 (outside the 40)\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; a=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/week/opening-articles-A2.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False)) for x in a if x[\\\"article\\\"] in [\\\"article O-5 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-2 (outside the 40)\\\",\\\"article O-4 (outside the 40)\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"article\": \"article O-2 (outside the 40)\", \"day\": \"2026-09-22\", \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\", \"title\": \"Top Analyst Reports for NVIDIA, Berkshire Hathaway & GE Aerospace\", \"text\": \"Tuesday, September 22, 2026\\nThe Zacks Research Daily presents the best research output of our analyst team. Today's Research Daily features new research reports on 16 major stocks, including NVIDIA Corp. (NVDA), Berkshire Hathaway Inc. (BRK.B) and GE Aerospace (GE). These research reports have been hand-picked from the roughly 70 reports published by our analyst team today.\\nYou can see all of today's research reports here >>>\\nThe daily 'Ahead of Wall Street' article is a must-read for all investors who would like to be ready for that day's trading action. The article comes out before the market opens, attempting to make sense of that morning's economic releases and how they will affect that day's market action. You can read this article for free on our home page and can actually sign up there to get an email notification as this article comes out each morning.\\nYou can read today's AWS here >>> Still Waters Bring Mixed Pre-Market Levels\\nShares of NVIDIA have gained +27.8% over the past year against the Zacks Semiconductor - General industry's gain of +35.6%. The company continues to benefit from broad demand for accelerated computing as customers build AI factories across hyperscalers, AI clouds, enterprises and sovereign buyers.\\nBlackwell Ultra is supporting growth while Vera Rubin broadens the platform across GPUs, CPUs, networking and software. Customer diversification, rising system content and usage-linked opportunities extend the runway, while cash generation supports capital returns. China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\\nCustomer concentration, rising operating investment and competition add uncertainty. Even so, platform leadership, product cadence and expanding AI infrastructure demand give the positive drivers greater weight in the current risk-reward profile.\\n(You can read the full research report on NVIDIA here >>> )\\nBerkshire Hathaway's shares have gained +1.6% over the past year against the Zacks Insurance - Property and Casualty industry's gain of +2.8%. The company benefits from diversified businesses, disciplined underwriting and a large liquidity base that support durable earnings capacity and flexible capital allocation. Insurance float remains a low-cost funding source, while manufacturing, services, BNSF and Berkshire Hathaway Energy broaden the earnings mix.\\nRecent acquisitions and renewed share repurchases show multiple avenues for deploying capital. Still, insurance results remain exposed to catastrophe losses and rising claim frequency and severity. Lower short-term rates are reducing insurance investment income, while BNSF and BHE require substantial capital spending.\\nGreg Abel is now leading the company, leaving execution under the new leadership structure as an important watchpoint. These offsets support an overall balanced risk-reward profile and a Neutral view.\\n(You can read the full research report on Berkshire Hathaway here >>> )\\nShares of GE Aerospace have outperformed the Zacks Aerospace - Defense industry over the past year (+4.9% vs. -13.2%). The company has been witnessing strength in its businesses, driven by robust demand for commercial engines, propulsion and additive technologies. Rising U.S. & international defense budgets, geopolitical tensions, positive airline & airframer dynamics and robust demand for commercial air travel augur well for the company.\\nGE Aerospace's portfolio-reshaping actions are likely to unlock value for its shareholders. The company raised its dividend by 30.6% to 36 cents per share in February 2026.\\nHowever, it has been dealing with high costs and expenses related to certain projects and restructuring activities, which are likely to affect its margins and profitability. Supply-chain disruptions may also result in delays and increased costs. Given GE Aerospace's international presence, foreign exchange headwinds might be worrying for the company.\\n(You can read the full research report on GE Aerospace here >>> )\\nOther noteworthy reports we are featuring today include Intel Corp. (INTC), Morgan Stanley (MS) and Arm Holdings plc (ARM).\\nPer the Zacks analyst, strong demand for AI and data-center infrastructure is likely to support Intel's top line. Its focus on AI-ready computing and advanced networking solutions is positive.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, rising adoption of compute platforms across cloud artificial intelligence is benefiting ARM. However, Elevated expenses due to increased investments remain a concern.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, Dollar Tree is benefiting from broad category growth, multi-price adoption and store improvements, while measured expansion and reinvestment support future sales.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, Textron gains from diversified exposure across business aviation, helicopters, defense platforms and aftermarket services, supported by strong demand and defense modernization.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, Antero Resources' expansion of its core West Virginia Marcellus position enhances its production outlook. However, its exposure to commodity price volatility raises concerns.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, Hexcel Corporation is likely to benefit from the improving air traffic. Yet labor shortage result in delays and likely impact operating results.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, SL Green benefits from strong leasing, rising rents and broad tenant demand. However, elevated debt, interest expenses and refinancing exposure remain material risks.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, solid IB business, strategic alliances/acquisitions, global footprint and Morgan Stanley's focus on less capital-markets-dependent operations will likely support its financials.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, growing 800G demand and 1.6T FiberEdge and CopperEdge ramps are driving Semtech's data center growth.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, strong Xifaxan growth, strategic acquisitions and new product approvals fuel Bausch Health's growth prospects. However, uncertainty around Bausch + Lomb separation is a concern.\\nThe Zacks analyst is concerned with Boston Scientific facing a sharp U.S. WATCHMAN market slowdown, as higher clinical evidence impacts referral trends. Portfolio gaps within CRM are also worrying.\\nPer the Zacks Analyst, Rigetti faces rising losses, execution and dilution risks, though cloud expansion, Novera sales, government ties and a solid cash position support longer-term potential.\\nPer the Zacks analyst, NVR faces housing affordability constraints, softer settlements and margin pressure. Also, lower home prices and higher lot costs remain concerns.\"}\n{\"article\": \"article O-4 (outside the 40)\", \"day\": \"2026-09-23\", \"publisher\": \"fool.com\", \"title\": \"I've Held Nvidia for Nearly 3 Years. Here's Whether I'd Still Buy Today.\", \"text\": \"Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has been one of the best stocks to own in the artificial intelligence (AI) arms race. It is up nearly 1,500% since 2023 began, providing investors with incredible returns. Unfortunately, I do not have those returns in my portfolio.\\nI bought the stock at the beginning of 2023 and watched it double. Then I sold the stock because I was worried that AI computing demand wouldn't last. I was very wrong. I had to disregard all of the prior gains and purchase shares in 2024 after it had run up some more, which has still been a great investment for me.\\nBut would I buy the stock again today? You bet I would.\\nNvidia is still a dominant force in the industry\\nNvidia is turning the investment world on its head. Normally, the law of large numbers dictates that the larger a company is, the harder it is to grow. Nvidia is the world's largest company and has reached heights that no other company has ever achieved. However, it's growing faster than any other stock among the world's top 10 companies. That's hard to believe, but it showcases just how massive the demand is for AI computing power.\\nNvidia makes GPUs , which are the go-to computing unit for all sorts of AI operations, be it training or inference. While there are alternatives, the Nvidia ecosystem cannot be beaten. Additionally, custom AI chips are starting to become more popular among many AI hyperscalers. Still, there will always be a place for computing units like Nvidia's GPUs that are flexible and can handle a wide variety of workloads.\\nDue to Nvidia's market position, it has advanced knowledge of how much computing power the AI hyperscalers are planning. The AI companies are making multiyear plans to ensure they have mapped out when they will need computing units, because data centers don't go up overnight. This gives Nvidia the ability to issue long-term guidance, and the guidance regarding the AI build-out is simply incredible.\\nNvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030. Considering the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year, that's huge growth still to come.\\nSo, if you think you've missed the boat on Nvidia, think again. There is still plenty of upside to come, and Nvidia will be one of the biggest beneficiaries of the AI build-out. However, none of it is priced into Nvidia's stock.\\nThat's pretty much in line with where other big tech stocks trade when one-time effects don't skew their valuations, but there's one problem: Nvidia is growing far faster than any of its big tech peers. The trailing price-to-earnings ratio doesn't account for Nvidia's massive growth ahead, and that's where a forward-looking metric helps out.\\nAt less than 15 times next year's earnings, Nvidia's stock has almost no growth priced into it today. That means the stock has major upside over the next year. Furthermore, with the AI build-out expected to intensify through 2030, that leaves plenty more room for Nvidia to run.\\nEven if you've missed out on Nvidia so far, now is the perfect time to buy more. Very little growth has been priced in, and Nvidia's stock looks well positioned to be one of the best investments over the next few years.\"}\n{\"article\": \"article O-5 (outside the 40)\", \"day\": \"2026-09-25\", \"publisher\": \"fool.com\", \"title\": \"Credo vs. Nvidia: Which AI Chip Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026?\", \"text\": \"The artificial intelligence boom has transformed the semiconductor landscape. Deciding between a high-growth connectivity specialist like Credo Technology Group ( CRDO +5.75% ) and the industry giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) requires a look at their specific niches.\\nBoth companies are central to modern data centers, but they solve different problems. Credo provides the high-speed wiring and connectivity chips that keep data moving, while Nvidia supplies the massive processing power AI models require. Comparing these two reveals how different players profit from the same infrastructure trend.\\nCredo designs high-speed copper and optical interconnect solutions essential for modern AI-driven infrastructure. The company operates as a niche provider of high-speed connectivity solutions among semiconductor stocks serving AI data centers. Credo relies on a limited number of customers for a substantial portion of its revenue, with sales to its top 10 customers accounting for approximately 90% of total revenue in fiscal 2026. This level of customer concentration adds risk to the business. The company also maintains a partnership with Oracle ( ORCL -0.13% ) to develop ZeroFlap optics to improve reliability in AI data centers.\\nFinancial performance has accelerated significantly recently. In fiscal 2026, which ended in May 2026, revenue reached nearly $1.3 billion, up roughly 205.7% from the previous year. This growth helped the company achieve a net income of approximately $472.3 million. The net margin for the period was approximately 35.4%, a substantial improvement from the 11.9% net margin reported in the prior fiscal year.\\nThe balance sheet remains highly liquid. As of its May 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio (a measure of short-term debt-paying ability) is nearly 10.2x. The debt-to-equity ratio (total debt compared to equity) is 0.0x, indicating no debt relative to equity. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) was nearly $407.0 million. Stock-based compensation represented roughly 39.3% of operating cash flow, which inflates reported cash generation because SBC is a non-cash expense added back in the cash flow statement.\\nNvidia is the dominant provider of GPU-accelerated computing platforms used in gaming, professional visualization, and data centers. The company sells its products to massive cloud service providers and AI model makers globally. Nvidia relies on a concentrated customer base, with sales to two direct customers representing 22% and 14% of total revenue in fiscal 2026, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds a layer of risk to the business. The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\\nRevenue growth remains a primary driver for the business. In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\\nStrong profitability supports a robust capital structure. As of its January 2026 balance sheet, the current ratio is approximately 3.9x. The debt-to-equity ratio (which compares total debt to shareholder equity) is under 0.1x, meaning the company carries very little debt. Free cash flow (cash from operations minus capital expenditures) reached nearly $96.7 billion for the fiscal year. This massive cash generation provides significant flexibility for research, development, and strategic acquisitions.\\nCredo faces intense competition from larger, well-established semiconductor companies and potential new entrants in the data infrastructure market. The company depends heavily on a small number of customers, making it vulnerable to losses or shifts in purchasing patterns. Its business model relies on third-party manufacturing partners, exposing the company to supply chain disruptions and geopolitical risks regarding operations in Taiwan and mainland China. Furthermore, evolving international trade controls and export restrictions could restrict its ability to serve key markets.\\nNvidia faces significant risks from global geopolitical tensions and export control regulations, particularly for high-performance AI chips. These restrictions have impacted sales to China and could continue to limit market access. The company faces intense competition from other hardware suppliers and internal development efforts by customers like Microsoft ( MSFT +1.22% ) , Amazon ( AMZN +0.51% ) , and Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.61% ) ( GOOG -0.77% ) . Nvidia also manages various legal challenges, including inquiries from competition regulators worldwide regarding its market practices.\\nNvidia trades at a lower forward P/E based on future earnings estimates, while both carry a high P/S ratio compared to historical averages.\\nI'd buy Nvidia, and it isn't close. Here's why:\\nCredo's growth is not the issue. Revenue in its latest quarter more than doubled from a year earlier, and management guided the current quarter higher still. The stock fell anyway because gross margin guidance came in flat, and shares now sit well below their 52-week high. That's the problem with a company that gets about 90% of its revenue from 10 customers. Any hint of slower spending from one of them moves the stock, and the valuation leaves no room for a miss.\\nNvidia is growing nearly as fast at a scale that makes Credo look like a rounding error. Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all. Nvidia has its own concentration risk, with two customers making up more than a third of sales, and the Hugging Face deal shows it's willing to spend to stay ahead.\\nFor a long-term investor, Nvidia offers the same AI infrastructure exposure at a lower price per dollar of expected earnings, with far less dependence on any single buyer. Credo belongs in a portfolio only as a small position you can afford to watch swing.\\nCredo more than tripled revenue last year but leans on 10 customers for 90% of sales, while Nvidia is doubling revenue at a scale nobody else in AI hardware can match.\\nBy Mike Schwenk – Updated Sep 25, 2026 at 3:00PM EST\\nCredo makes the high-speed connectivity chips and cables that move data between servers inside AI data centers, and revenue more than tripled in fiscal 2026.\\nNvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\\nBoth companies depend on a small group of customers, but Nvidia's scale, margins, and lower valuation make it the stronger buy today.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"part\":1,\"matter\":\"M01\",\"system\":\"X\",\"status\":\"missed\",\"main_outcome\":null,\"ids\":[],\"note\":\"No FY2027 revenue question.\"}\n{\"part\":1,\"matter\":\"M01\",\"system\":\"Y\",\"status\":\"opened\",\"main_outcome\":null,\"ids\":[\"Y-2\",\"Y-4\"],\"note\":\"Total revenue and the dominant data-center segment are both covered.\"}\n{\"part\":1,\"matter\":\"M02\",\"system\":\"X\",\"status\":\"missed\",\"main_outcome\":null,\"ids\":[],\"note\":\"No FY2028 revenue question.\"}\n{\"part\":1,\"matter\":\"M02\",\"system\":\"Y\",\"status\":\"opened\",\"main_outcome\":null,\"ids\":[\"Y-3\",\"Y-5\"],\"note\":\"Total revenue and its growth describe the same annual revenue outcome.\"}\n{\"part\":1,\"matter\":\"M03\",\"system\":\"X\",\"status\":\"missed\",\"main_outcome\":null,\"ids\":[],\"note\":\"No FY2028 gross-margin question.\"}\n{\"part\":1,\"matter\":\"M03\",\"system\":\"Y\",\"status\":\"opened\",\"main_outcome\":null,\"ids\":[\"Y-10\"],\"note\":\"The opening article frames the question against the 72%–73% outlook.\"}\n{\"part\":1,\"matter\":\"M04\",\"system\":\"X\",\"status\":\"opened\",\"main_outcome\":null,\"ids\":[\"X-2\"],\"note\":\"The next annual report covers FY2027 purchase and supply commitments.\"}\n{\"part\":1,\"matter\":\"M04\",\"system\":\"Y\",\"status\":\"missed\",\"main_outcome\":null,\"ids\":[],\"note\":\"The acquisition question does not ask for NVIDIA's overall supply commitments.\"}\n{\"part\":1,\"matter\":\"M05\",\"system\":\"X\",\"status\":\"opened\",\"main_outcome\":null,\"ids\":[\"X-7\"],\"note\":\"Asks for Chinese approval of the named purchases by fiscal year-end.\"}\n{\"part\":1,\"matter\":\"M05\",\"system\":\"Y\",\"status\":\"opened\",\"main_outcome\":null,\"ids\":[\"Y-14\",\"Y-15\"],\"note\":\"Both ask about approval of the same purchases by January 31.\"}\n{\"part\":1,\"matter\":\"M06\",\"system\":\"X\",\"status\":\"missed\",\"main_outcome\":null,\"ids\":[],\"note\":\"No FY2027 gross-margin question.\"}\n{\"part\":1,\"matter\":\"M06\",\"system\":\"Y\",\"status\":\"missed\",\"main_outcome\":null,\"ids\":[],\"note\":\"Y-10 asks about FY2028, not FY2027.\"}\n{\"part\":1,\"matter\":\"M07\",\"system\":\"X\",\"status\":\"broader\",\"main_outcome\":false,\"ids\":[\"X-4\"],\"note\":\"Shipments by fiscal year-end could begin after Q3, so the answer does not establish the Q3 outcome.\"}\n{\"part\":1,\"matter\":\"M07\",\"system\":\"Y\",\"status\":\"broader\",\"main_outcome\":false,\"ids\":[\"Y-9\"],\"note\":\"Shipments by January could begin after Q3, so the answer does not establish the Q3 outcome.\"}\n{\"part\":1,\"matter\":\"M08\",\"system\":\"X\",\"status\":\"missed\",\"main_outcome\":null,\"ids\":[],\"note\":\"No AMD data-center revenue question.\"}\n{\"part\":1,\"matter\":\"M08\",\"system\":\"Y\",\"status\":\"missed\",\"main_outcome\":null,\"ids\":[],\"note\":\"No AMD data-center revenue question.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"X-1\",\"verdict\":\"worth\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"Large customer credit support and guarantees can change NVIDIA's credit risk; annual disclosures provide a dated check.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"X-2\",\"verdict\":\"worth\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"Growing supply obligations can materially affect cash and supply risk; the annual report can disclose their amount.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"X-3\",\"verdict\":\"worth\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"Access to China's AI-chip market can materially affect sales; US and Chinese decisions can be checked by fiscal year-end.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"X-4\",\"verdict\":\"worth\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"Revenue-bearing shipments of NVIDIA's next platform are a major product milestone, checkable through company disclosures.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"X-5\",\"verdict\":\"worth\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"Combined hyperscaler infrastructure spending affects demand across suppliers and can be checked against annual disclosures.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"X-6\",\"verdict\":\"trivia\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"The $12.9 billion deal is small relative to NVIDIA's scale and cash generation; the article does not establish a position-changing impact.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"X-7\",\"verdict\":\"worth\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"A Chinese approval could change NVIDIA's market access and competitive position; the pending decision has a fiscal year-end check date.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"Y-1\",\"verdict\":\"worth\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"The scale of customer financing and guarantees can change credit risk; the annual report supplies a dated check.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"Y-2\",\"verdict\":\"worth\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"FY2027 revenue is central to NVIDIA's valuation and can be checked in its annual results.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"Y-3\",\"verdict\":\"worth\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"FY2028 revenue is central to the growth outlook and can be checked in annual results.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"Y-4\",\"verdict\":\"worth\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"Data-center revenue is NVIDIA's dominant business and reflects a distinct cloud-versus-local demand question; annual results disclose it.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"Y-5\",\"verdict\":\"duplicate\",\"duplicate_of\":\"Y-3\",\"why\":\"FY2028 revenue growth and the FY2028 revenue amount resolve the same annual outcome once the prior-year revenue is known.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"Y-6\",\"verdict\":\"worth\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"US permission for Blackwell exports could materially change access to China; a regulatory decision can be checked by the specified date.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"Y-7\",\"verdict\":\"worth\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"The five hyperscalers' combined data-center spending is a major demand driver, checkable against their public disclosures.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"Y-8\",\"verdict\":\"trivia\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"The question leaves both the companies and AI spending undefined, so no identifiable public measure can settle its stated total.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"Y-9\",\"verdict\":\"worth\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"The start of shipments for NVIDIA's next major platform can affect revenue and be checked through company announcements.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"Y-10\",\"verdict\":\"worth\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"FY2028 gross margin affects the earnings behind NVIDIA's valuation and is checkable in annual results.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"Y-11\",\"verdict\":\"duplicate\",\"duplicate_of\":\"Y-9\",\"why\":\"This asks again when the same Rubin generation begins shipping; the later deadline belongs with the earlier open question.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"Y-12\",\"verdict\":\"trivia\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"The article gives an acquisition price, not a purchase commitment disclosed separately in the commitments note; that specific amount may not be publicly reported.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"Y-13\",\"verdict\":\"trivia\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"The article describes the potential $10 billion investment as small relative to NVIDIA's profits and says it would not change much by itself.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"Y-14\",\"verdict\":\"worth\",\"duplicate_of\":null,\"why\":\"Chinese permission for these GPU purchases can affect NVIDIA's market access and competition; the decision can be checked by the stated date.\"}\n{\"part\":2,\"id\":\"Y-15\",\"verdict\":\"duplicate\",\"duplicate_of\":\"Y-14\",\"why\":\"Both ask whether MIIT approves domestic firms' RTX Pro 5500 purchases by January 31; the named companies are examples of those firms.\"}"}}
